{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06005","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06005","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06005","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qin","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Qin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuntian","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yuntian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heming","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Heming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhang","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yunhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohan","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Bohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianyue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lianyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiao","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Jiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaohui","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Shaohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongrong","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Rongrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MASA: Rethinking the Representational Bottleneck in LoRA with Multi-A Shared Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:06:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:53:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-09T09:29:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T02:39:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-01T11:39:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:10:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) has emerged as a dominant method in Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) for large language models, which augments the transformer layer with one down-projection $A$ and one up-projection $B$. However, LoRA's reliance on a single down-projection matrix ($A$) creates a representational bottleneck, as this solitary feature extractor is inherently insufficient for capturing the diverse signals required by complex tasks. This motivates our architectural shift to focus on enriching the feature adaptation to improve the downstream task adaptation ability. We propose MASA (Multi-$A$ Shared Adaptation), an architecture that implements a multi-$A$, single-$B$ structure where the multi-$A$ expert ensemble is asymmetrically shared across layers to ensure parameter efficiency. In MASA, these specialized experts capture diverse features, which are then integrated by a single, layer-specific $B$-matrix. The effectiveness and versatility of our method are validated through a comprehensive suite of experiments spanning multi-domain generalization, single-domain specialization, and multi-task reasoning. For example, on the MMLU benchmark, MASA achieves an average accuracy of 59.62%, outperforming the standard LoRA by 1.08 points (a relative improvement of 1.84%) with comparable learnable parameters of 0.52%."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06005","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:02:09Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:02:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05534","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05534","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05534","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jung-jae","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jung-jae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roy Ka-wei","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Roy Ka-wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lidong","familyName":"Bing","name":"Bing, Lidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Revisiting Self-Play Preference Optimization: On the Role of Prompt Difficulty"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T02:47:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:20:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T03:59:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:22:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T08:21:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:48:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Self-play preference optimization has emerged as a prominent paradigm for aligning large language models (LLMs). It typically involves a language model to generate on-policy responses for prompts and a reward model (RM) to guide the selection of chosen and rejected responses, which can be further trained with direct preference optimization (DPO). However, the role of prompts remains underexplored, despite being a core component in this pipeline. In this work, we investigate how prompts of varying difficulty influence self-play preference optimization. We use the mean reward of\n sampled responses of a prompt as a proxy for its difficulty. We first find that difficult prompts exhibit substantially inferior self-play optimization performance compared to easy prompts for language models. Moreover, incorporating difficult prompts into training fails to enhance overall performance and, in fact, leads to slight degradation compared to training on easy prompts alone. Third, there is a clear upward trend in optimization performance as prompt difficulty decreases. We also observe that the performance gap between difficult and easy prompts tends to close as the model capacity increases, suggesting that prompt difficulty interacts with the model capacity. Building on these findings, we explore strategies to mitigate the adversary effect of difficult prompts on final performance. We demonstrate that only training on a small portion (30%) of the easiest prompts improves overall self-play performance on AlpacaEval~2 and Arena-Hard. We also report failed attempts and lessons learned."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05534","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:51:06Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:51:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05132","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05132","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05132","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiva Prasad","familyName":"Kasiviswanathan","name":"Kasiviswanathan, Shiva Prasad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Training Large Language Models To Reason In Parallel With Global Forking Tokens"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T02:48:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-06T07:00:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-07T01:25:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T00:48:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:30:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although LLMs have demonstrated improved performance by scaling parallel test-time compute, doing so relies on generating reasoning paths that are both diverse and accurate. For challenging problems, the forking tokens that trigger diverse yet correct reasoning modes are typically deep in the sampling tree. Consequently, common strategies to encourage diversity, such as temperature scaling, encounter a worsened trade-off between diversity and accuracy. Motivated by this challenge, we treat parallel reasoning as a set-of-next-token-prediction problem and incorporate a set-based global loss into Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) using bipartite matching between global forking tokens and unique reasoning traces. We observe that whereas naive fine-tuning with multiple reasoning traces collapses these unique reasoning modes, our proposed method, Set Supervised Fine-Tuning (SSFT), preserves these modes and produces emergent global forking tokens. Global Forking Policy Optimization (GFPO) leverages these maximally steerable tokens to incentivize complex reasoning, and the resulting models consistently outperform their SFT counterparts with GRPO on both math reasoning and execution-based code generation benchmarks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05132","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:49Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05069","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05069","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05069","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dachuan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Dachuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abedelkadir","familyName":"Asi","name":"Asi, Abedelkadir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keying","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Keying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangchi","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Xiangchi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leyan","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Leyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenke","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Wenke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SwiReasoning: Switch-Thinking in Latent and Explicit for Pareto-Superior Reasoning LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:46:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-06T16:34:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:33:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-01T18:32:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:21:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent work shows that, beyond discrete reasoning through explicit chain-of-thought steps, which are limited by the boundaries of natural languages, large language models (LLMs) can also reason continuously in latent space, allowing richer information per step and thereby improving token efficiency. Despite this promise, latent reasoning still faces two challenges, especially in training-free settings: 1) purely latent reasoning broadens the search distribution by maintaining multiple implicit paths, which diffuses probability mass, introduces noise, and impedes convergence to a single high-confidence solution, thereby hurting accuracy; and 2) overthinking persists even without explicit text, wasting tokens and degrading efficiency. To address these issues, we introduce SwiReasoning, a training-free framework for LLM reasoning which features two key innovations: 1) SwiReasoning dynamically switches between explicit and latent reasoning, guided by block-wise confidence estimated from entropy trends in next-token distributions, to balance exploration and exploitation and promote timely convergence. 2) By limiting the maximum number of thinking-block switches, SwiReasoning curbs overthinking and improves token efficiency across varying problem difficulties. On widely used mathematics, STEM, coding, and general benchmarks, SwiReasoning consistently improves average accuracy by 1.8%-3.1% across reasoning LLMs of different model families and scales. Furthermore, under constrained budgets, SwiReasoning improves average token efficiency by 57%-79%, with larger gains as budgets tighten."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026. Code: https://github.com/sdc17/SwiReasoning, Website: https://swireasoning.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05069","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:05:00Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:05:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05064","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05064","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05064","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sara","familyName":"Kangaslahti","name":"Kangaslahti, Sara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nihal V.","familyName":"Nayak","name":"Nayak, Nihal V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Geuter","name":"Geuter, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Fumero","name":"Fumero, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Locatello","name":"Locatello, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Alvarez-Melis","name":"Alvarez-Melis, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Boomerang Distillation Enables Zero-Shot Model Size Interpolation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:41:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T21:21:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:12:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are typically deployed under diverse memory and compute constraints. Existing approaches build model families by training each size independently, which is prohibitively expensive and provides only coarse-grained size options. In this work, we identify a novel phenomenon that we call boomerang distillation: starting from a large base model (the teacher), one first distills down to a small student and then progressively reconstructs intermediate-sized models by re-incorporating blocks of teacher layers into the student without any additional training. This process produces zero-shot interpolated models of many intermediate sizes whose performance scales smoothly between the student and teacher, often matching or surpassing pretrained or distilled models of the same size. We further analyze when this type of interpolation succeeds, showing that alignment between teacher and student through pruning and distillation is essential. Boomerang distillation thus provides a simple and efficient way to generate fine-grained model families, dramatically reducing training cost while enabling flexible adaptation across deployment environments. The code and models are available at https://github.com/dcml-lab/boomerang-distillation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05064","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:04:47Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:04:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05930","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05930","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05930","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Bamberger","name":"Bamberger, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iolo","familyName":"Jones","name":"Jones, Iolo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dennis","familyName":"Duncan","name":"Duncan, Dennis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael M.","familyName":"Bronstein","name":"Bronstein, Michael M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pierre","familyName":"Vandergheynst","name":"Vandergheynst, Pierre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Gosztolai","name":"Gosztolai, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Carré du champ flow matching: better quality-generalisation tradeoff in generative models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Differential Geometry (math.DG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:41:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:49:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T17:23:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:58:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep generative models often face a fundamental tradeoff: high sample quality can come at the cost of memorisation, where the model reproduces training data rather than generalising across the underlying data geometry. We introduce Carré du champ flow matching (CDC-FM), a generalisation of flow matching (FM), that improves the quality-generalisation tradeoff by regularising the probability path with a geometry-aware noise. Our method replaces the homogeneous, isotropic noise in FM with a spatially varying, anisotropic Gaussian noise whose covariance captures the local geometry of the latent data manifold. We prove that this geometric noise can be optimally estimated from the data and is scalable to large data. Further, we provide an extensive experimental evaluation on diverse datasets (synthetic manifolds, point clouds, single-cell genomics, animal motion capture, and images) as well as various neural network architectures (MLPs, CNNs, and transformers). We demonstrate that CDC-FM consistently offers a better quality-generalisation tradeoff. We observe significant improvements over standard FM in data-scarce regimes and in highly non-uniformly sampled datasets, which are often encountered in AI for science applications. Our work provides a mathematical framework for studying the interplay between data geometry, generalisation and memorisation in generative models, as well as a robust and scalable algorithm that can be readily integrated into existing flow matching pipelines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05930","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:00:22Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:00:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:07:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05228","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05228","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05228","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haining","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Haining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James V.","familyName":"Roggeveen","name":"Roggeveen, James V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erez","familyName":"Berg","name":"Berg, Erez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Carrasquilla","name":"Carrasquilla, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debanjan","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Debanjan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Surya","familyName":"Ganguli","name":"Ganguli, Surya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federico","familyName":"Ghimenti","name":"Ghimenti, Federico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juraj","familyName":"Hasik","name":"Hasik, Juraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Hunt","name":"Hunt, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong-Chen","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Hong-Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mason","familyName":"Kamb","name":"Kamb, Mason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying-Jer","familyName":"Kao","name":"Kao, Ying-Jer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ehsan","familyName":"Khatami","name":"Khatami, Ehsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael J.","familyName":"Lawler","name":"Lawler, Michael J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Titus","familyName":"Neupert","name":"Neupert, Titus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoliang","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Xiaoliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael P.","familyName":"Brenner","name":"Brenner, Michael P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eun-Ah","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Eun-Ah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CMT-Benchmark: A Benchmark for Condensed Matter Theory Built by Expert Researchers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:00:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-10T01:22:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T16:22:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:54:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have shown remarkable progress in coding and math problem-solving, but evaluation on advanced research-level problems in hard sciences remains scarce. To fill this gap, we present CMT-Benchmark, a dataset of 50 problems covering condensed matter theory (CMT) at the level of an expert researcher. Topics span analytical and computational approaches in quantum many-body, and classical statistical mechanics. The dataset was designed and verified by a panel of expert researchers from around the world. We built the dataset through a collaborative environment that challenges the panel to write and refine problems they would want a research assistant to solve, including Hartree-Fock, exact diagonalization, quantum/variational Monte Carlo, density matrix renormalization group (DMRG), quantum/classical statistical mechanics, and model building. We evaluate LLMs by programmatically checking solutions against expert-supplied ground truth. We developed machine-grading, including symbolic handling of non-commuting operators via normal ordering. They generalize across tasks too. Our evaluations show that frontier models struggle with all of the problems in the dataset, highlighting a gap in the physical reasoning skills of current LLMs. Notably, experts identified strategies for creating increasingly difficult problems by interacting with the LLMs and exploiting common failure modes. The best model, GPT5, solves 30\\% of the problems; average across 17 models (GPT, Gemini, Claude, DeepSeek, Llama) is 11.4\\pm2.1\\%. Moreover, 18 problems are solved by none of the 17 models, and 26 by at most one. These unsolved problems span Quantum Monte Carlo, Variational Monte Carlo, and DMRG. Answers sometimes violate fundamental symmetries or have unphysical scaling dimensions. We believe this benchmark will guide development toward capable AI research assistants and tutors."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CMT-Benchmark dataset is available at https://huggingface.co/datasets/JVRoggeveen/cmt_benchmark. CMT-Benchmark was referenced in the Gemini 3 Deep Think (February 2026) release at https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/gemini-3-deep-think/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05228","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:44:03Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:44:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:07:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06008","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06008","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06008","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moritz","familyName":"Alker","name":"Alker, Moritz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David C.","familyName":"Schedl","name":"Schedl, David C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Stöckl","name":"Stöckl, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Detection and Measurement of Hailstones with Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.4; I.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07, 68T45, 86A10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:07:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:38:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/icecer65523.2025.11400929","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study examines the use of social media and news images to detect and measure hailstones, utilizing pre-trained multimodal large language models. The dataset for this study comprises 474 crowdsourced images of hailstones from documented hail events in Austria, which occurred between January 2022 and September 2024. These hailstones have maximum diameters ranging from 2 to 11cm. We estimate the hail diameters and compare four different models utilizing one-stage and two-stage prompting strategies. The latter utilizes additional size cues from reference objects, such as human hands, within the image. Our results show that pretrained models already have the potential to measure hailstone diameters from images with an average mean absolute error of 1.12cm for the best model. In comparison to a single-stage prompt, two-stage prompting improves the reliability of most models. Our study suggests that these off-the-shelf models, even without fine-tuning, can complement traditional hail sensors by extracting meaningful and spatially dense information from social media imagery, enabling faster and more detailed assessments of severe weather events. The automated real-time image harvesting from social media and other sources remains an open task, but it will make our approach directly applicable to future hail events."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 5 figures, accepted at The 2nd International Conference on Electrical and Computer Engineering Researches"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06008","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T02:02:13Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:02:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:15:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05077","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05077","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05077","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zishen","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Zishen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emma","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Emma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqiang","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Zhiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tushar","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Tushar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijay Janapa","familyName":"Reddi","name":"Reddi, Vijay Janapa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Slm-mux: Orchestrating small language models for reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:49:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T06:48:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:30:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid development of language models, the number of small language models (SLMs) has grown significantly. Although they do not achieve state-of-the-art accuracy, they are more efficient and often excel at specific tasks. This raises a natural question: can multiple SLMs be orchestrated into a system where each contributes effectively, achieving higher accuracy than any individual model? Existing orchestration methods have primarily targeted frontier models (e.g., GPT-4) and perform suboptimally when applied to SLMs. To address this gap, we propose a three-stage approach for orchestrating SLMs. First, we introduce SLM-MUX, a multi-model architecture that effectively coordinates multiple SLMs. Building on this, we develop two optimization strategies: (i) a model selection search that identifies the most complementary SLMs from a given pool, and (ii) test-time scaling tailored to SLM-MUX. Our approach delivers strong results: Compared to existing orchestration methods, our approach achieves up to 13.4% improvement on MATH, 8.8% on GPQA, and 7.0% on GSM8K. With just two SLMs, SLM-MUX outperforms Qwen 2.5 72B on GPQA and GSM8K, and matches its performance on MATH. We further provide theoretical analyses to substantiate the advantages of our method. Additional experiments show that the core principle of SLM-MUX extends to open-ended generation tasks (e.g., HumanEval) and benefits other model classes, including frontier LLMs and domain-specific fine-tuned SLMs. In summary, we demonstrate that SLMs can be effectively orchestrated into more accurate and efficient systems through the proposed approach. The project page is available at https://slm-mux.github.io/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05077","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:05:19Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:05:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:26:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05598","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05598","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05598","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingdai","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Mingdai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nurendra","familyName":"Choudhary","name":"Choudhary, Nurendra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangshu","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Jiangshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edward W.","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Edward W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philip S.","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Philip S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthik","familyName":"Subbian","name":"Subbian, Karthik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danai","familyName":"Koutra","name":"Koutra, Danai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentDR: Dynamic Recommendation with Implicit Item-Item Relations via LLM-based Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T05:48:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:26:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-22T15:17:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:52:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T12:32:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:45:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3774904.3792304","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent agent-based recommendation frameworks aim to simulate user behaviors by incorporating memory mechanisms and prompting strategies, but they struggle with hallucinating non-existent items and full-catalog ranking. Besides, a largely underexplored opportunity lies in leveraging LLMs'commonsense reasoning to capture user intent through substitute and complement relationships between items, which are usually implicit in datasets and difficult for traditional ID-based recommenders to capture. In this work, we propose a novel LLM-agent framework, AgenDR, which bridges LLM reasoning with scalable recommendation tools. Our approach delegates full-ranking tasks to traditional models while utilizing LLMs to (i) integrate multiple recommendation outputs based on personalized tool suitability and (ii) reason over substitute and complement relationships grounded in user history. This design mitigates hallucination, scales to large catalogs, and enhances recommendation relevance through relational reasoning. Through extensive experiments on three public grocery datasets, we show that our framework achieves superior full-ranking performance, yielding on average a twofold improvement over its underlying tools. We also introduce a new LLM-based evaluation metric that jointly measures semantic alignment and ranking correctness."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, accepted by WWW'26 as long paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05598","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:36Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T03:09:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05761","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05761","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05761","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sedat","familyName":"Dogan","name":"Dogan, Sedat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nina","familyName":"Dethlefs","name":"Dethlefs, Nina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debarati","familyName":"Chakraborty","name":"Chakraborty, Debarati","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Early Multimodal Prediction of Cross-Lingual Meme Virality on Reddit: A Time-Window Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:27:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:39:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-21T16:44:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:31:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Memes are a central part of online culture, yet their virality remains difficult to predict, especially in cross-lingual settings. We present a large-scale, time-series dataset of 46,578 Reddit memes collected from 25 meme-centric subreddits across eight language groups, with more than one million engagement tracking points. We propose a data-driven definition of virality based on a Hybrid Score that normalises engagement by community size and integrates dynamic features such as velocity and acceleration. This approach directly addresses the field's reliance on static, simple volume-based thresholds with arbitrary cut-offs. Building on this target, we construct a multimodal feature set that combines Visual, Textual, Contextual, Network, and Temporal signals, including structured annotations from a multimodal LLM to scale cross-lingual content labelling in a consistent way. We benchmark interpretable baselines (XGBoost, MLP) against end-to-end deep models (BERT, InceptionV3, CLIP) across early observation windows from 30 to 420 minutes. Our best model, a multimodal XGBoost classifier, achieves a PR AUC of 0.43 at 30 minutes and 0.80 at 420 minutes, indicating that early prediction of meme virality is feasible even under strong class imbalance. The results reveal a clear Content Ceiling, where content-only and deep multimodal baselines plateau at low PR AUC, while structural Network and Temporal features are necessary to surpass this limit. A SHAP-based temporal analysis further uncovers an evidentiary transition, where early predictions are dominated by network priors (author and community context), and later predictions increasingly rely on temporal dynamics (velocity, acceleration) as engagement accumulates. Overall, we reframe meme virality as a dynamic, path-dependent process governed by exposure and early interaction patterns rather than by intrinsic content alone."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ACM WebSci 2026. 10 pages, 9 fiures and 8 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05761","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:56:23Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:56:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04891","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04891","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04891","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Punya Syon","familyName":"Pandey","name":"Pandey, Punya Syon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hai Son","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Hai Son","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Devansh","familyName":"Bhardwaj","name":"Bhardwaj, Devansh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rada","familyName":"Mihalcea","name":"Mihalcea, Rada","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijing","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Zhijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SocialHarmBench: Revealing LLM Vulnerabilities to Socially Harmful Requests"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:11:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:39:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-22T19:11:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:57:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in contexts where their failures can have direct sociopolitical consequences. Yet, existing safety benchmarks rarely test vulnerabilities in domains such as political manipulation, propaganda and disinformation generation, or surveillance and information control. We introduce SocialHarmBench, a dataset of 585 prompts spanning 7 sociopolitical categories and 34 countries, designed to surface where LLMs most acutely fail in politically charged contexts. Our evaluations reveal several shortcomings: open-weight models exhibit high vulnerability to harmful compliance, with Mistral-7B reaching attack success rates as high as 97% to 98% in domains such as historical revisionism, propaganda, and political manipulation. Moreover, temporal and geographic analyses show that LLMs are most fragile when confronted with 21st-century or pre-20th-century contexts, and when responding to prompts tied to regions such as Latin America, the USA, and the UK. These findings demonstrate that current safeguards fail to generalize to high-stakes sociopolitical settings, exposing systematic biases and raising concerns about the reliability of LLMs in preserving human rights and democratic values. We share the SocialHarmBench benchmark at https://huggingface.co/datasets/psyonp/SocialHarmBench."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04891","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:57:37Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:57:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06170","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06170","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06170","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naveenkumar G","familyName":"Venkataswamy","name":"Venkataswamy, Naveenkumar G","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soumyabrata","familyName":"Dey","name":"Dey, Soumyabrata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephanie","familyName":"Schuckers","name":"Schuckers, Stephanie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masudul H","familyName":"Imtiaz","name":"Imtiaz, Masudul H","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Smartphone-based iris recognition through high-quality visible-spectrum iris image capture.V2"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:33:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:01:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T16:44:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:54:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-20T15:00:19Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v3; The new version is available at arXiv:2512.15548"},{"date":"2026-02-23T01:39:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Smartphone-based iris recognition in the visible spectrum (VIS) remains difficult due to illumination variability, pigmentation differences, and the absence of standardized capture controls. This work presents a compact end-to-end pipeline that enforces ISO/IEC 29794-6 quality compliance at acquisition and demonstrates that accurate VIS iris recognition is feasible on commodity devices. Using a custom Android application performing real-time framing, sharpness evaluation, and feedback, we introduce the CUVIRIS dataset of 752 compliant images from 47 subjects. A lightweight MobileNetV3-based multi-task segmentation network (LightIrisNet) is developed for efficient on-device processing, and a transformer matcher (IrisFormer) is adapted to the VIS domain. Under a standardized protocol and comparative benchmarking against prior CNN baselines, OSIRIS attains a TAR of 97.9% at FAR=0.01 (EER=0.76%), while IrisFormer, trained only on UBIRIS.v2, achieves an EER of 0.057% on CUVIRIS. The acquisition app, trained models, and a public subset of the dataset are released to support reproducibility. These results confirm that standardized capture and VIS-adapted lightweight models enable accurate and practical iris recognition on smartphones."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The new version is available at arXiv:2512.15548"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06170","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:00Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-23T02:45:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05703","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05703","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05703","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihan","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seo Taek","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Seo Taek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R.","familyName":"Srikant","name":"Srikant, R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Provably Convergent Primal-Dual DPO for Constrained LLM Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T09:10:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:34:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-12T09:59:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:40:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The widespread application of large language models (LLMs) raises increasing demands on ensuring safety or imposing constraints, such as reducing harmful content and adhering to predefined rules. While there have been several works studying LLM safety alignment, these works either need to train three models and incur high memory costs, or require prior knowledge on the optimal solution. Witnessing this fact, we investigate the constrained alignment problem for LLMs, i.e., maximizing the reward of outputs while restricting the cost to stay below a threshold. We propose a novel primal-dual direct preference optimization (DPO) approach, which first trains a model using standard DPO on reward preference data to provide reward information, and then adopts a rearranged Lagrangian DPO objective utilizing the provided reward information to fine-tune LLMs. Our approach only needs to train two models rather than three, which significantly saves memory costs, and does not require extra prior knowledge. Moreover, we establish rigorous suboptimality and constraint violation guarantees. We also extend our approach to enable online exploration and drop the data coverage dependence in the results. Experiments on the PKU-SafeRLHF and TruthfulQA datasets demonstrate the state-of-the-art performance of our approach."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05703","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:55:02Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:55:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-13T03:21:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04899","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04899","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04899","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keane","familyName":"Ong","name":"Ong, Keane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carol","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Carol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dewei","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Dewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengzhi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hengzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jingyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaee","familyName":"Cheong","name":"Cheong, Jiaee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gianmarco","familyName":"Mengaldo","name":"Mengaldo, Gianmarco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erik","familyName":"Cambria","name":"Cambria, Erik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul Pu","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Paul Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Human Behavior Atlas: Benchmarking Unified Psychological and Social Behavior Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:16:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:40:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-12T10:38:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:42:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Using intelligent systems to perceive psychological and social behaviors, that is, the underlying affective, cognitive, and pathological states that are manifested through observable behaviors and social interactions, remains a challenge due to their complex, multifaceted, and personalized nature. Existing work tackling these dimensions through specialized datasets and single-task systems often miss opportunities for scalability, cross-task transfer, and broader generalization. To address this gap, we curate Human Behavior Atlas, a unified benchmark of diverse behavioral tasks designed to support the development of foundation models for understanding psychological and social behaviors. Human Behavior Atlas comprises over 100,000 samples spanning text, audio, and visual modalities, covering tasks on affective states, cognitive states, pathologies, and social processes. Our unification efforts can reduce redundancy and cost, enable training to scale efficiently across tasks, and enhance generalization of behavioral features across domains. On Human Behavior Atlas, we train three models: Omnisapiens-7B SFT, Omnisapiens-7B BAM, and Omnisapiens-7B RL. We show that training on Human Behavior Atlas enables models to consistently outperform existing multimodal LLMs across diverse behavioral tasks. Pretraining on Human Behavior Atlas also improves transfer to novel behavioral datasets; with the targeted use of behavioral descriptors yielding meaningful performance gains. The benchmark, models, and codes can be found at: https://github.com/MIT-MI/human_behavior_atlas."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026 Main Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04899","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:57:56Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:57:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-13T03:21:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04860","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04860","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04860","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siwei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Siwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiwen","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Kaiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaofeng","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Yaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cihang","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Cihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohit","familyName":"Bansal","name":"Bansal, Mohit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linjun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaxiu","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Huaxiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Alignment Tipping Process: How Self-Evolution Pushes LLM Agents Off the Rails"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:48:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:38:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-11T20:59:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:08:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As Large Language Model (LLM) agents increasingly gain self-evolutionary capabilities to adapt and refine their strategies through real-world interaction, their long-term reliability becomes a critical concern. We identify the Alignment Tipping Process (ATP), a critical post-deployment risk unique to self-evolving LLM agents. Unlike training-time failures, ATP arises when continual interaction drives agents to abandon alignment constraints established during training in favor of reinforced, self-interested strategies. We formalize and analyze ATP through two complementary paradigms: Self-Interested Exploration, where repeated high-reward deviations induce individual behavioral drift, and Imitative Strategy Diffusion, where deviant behaviors spread across multi-agent systems. Building on these paradigms, we construct controllable testbeds and benchmark both open and closed-source LLMs. Our experiments show that alignment benefits erode rapidly under self-evolution, with initially aligned models converging toward unaligned states. In multi-agent settings, successful violations diffuse quickly, leading to collective misalignment. Moreover, current reinforcement learning-based alignment methods provide limited defenses against alignment tipping. These findings demonstrate that alignment of LLM agents is not a static property but a fragile and dynamic one, vulnerable to feedback-driven decay during deployment. Our data and code are available at https://github.com/aiming-lab/ATP."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04860","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:56:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:56:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-13T03:21:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05846","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05846","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05846","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maxence","familyName":"Lasbordes","name":"Lasbordes, Maxence","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sinoué","familyName":"Gad","name":"Gad, Sinoué","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Luth: Efficient French Specialization for Small Language Models and Cross-Lingual Transfer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:08:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:43:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-09T10:52:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T02:44:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The landscape of Large Language Models remains predominantly English-centric, resulting in a significant performance gap for other major languages, such as French, especially in the context of Small Language Models (SLMs). Existing multilingual models demonstrate considerably lower performance in French compared to English, and research on efficient adaptation methods for French remains limited. To address this, we introduce \\textbf{Luth}, a family of French-specialized SLMs: through targeted post-training on curated, high-quality French data, our models outperform all open-source counterparts of comparable size on multiple French benchmarks while retaining their original English capabilities. We further show that strategic model merging enhances performance in both languages, establishing Luth as a new state of the art for French SLMs and a robust baseline for future French-language research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the EACL 2026 Student Research Workshop (SRW)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05846","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:58:23Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:58:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-10T05:21:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05742","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05742","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05742","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanwei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yanwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wesley Hanwen","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Wesley Hanwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sijia","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Sijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Motahhare","familyName":"Eslami","name":"Eslami, Motahhare","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason I.","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Jason I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arpit","familyName":"Narechania","name":"Narechania, Arpit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Perer","name":"Perer, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vipera: Blending Visual and LLM-Driven Guidance for Systematic Auditing of Text-to-Image Generative AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:01:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:38:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-03T09:31:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-04T01:46:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3772318.3791942","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite their increasing capabilities, text-to-image generative AI systems are known to produce biased, offensive, and otherwise problematic outputs. While recent advancements have supported testing and auditing of generative AI, existing auditing methods still face challenges in supporting effectively explore the vast space of AI-generated outputs in a structured way. To address this gap, we conducted formative studies with five AI auditors and synthesized five design goals for supporting systematic AI audits. Based on these insights, we developed Vipera, an interactive auditing interface that employs multiple visual cues including a scene graph to facilitate image sensemaking and inspire auditors to explore and hierarchically organize the auditing criteria. Additionally, Vipera leverages LLM-powered suggestions to facilitate exploration of unexplored auditing directions. Through a controlled experiment with 24 participants experienced in AI auditing, we demonstrate Vipera's effectiveness in helping auditors navigate large AI output spaces and organize their analyses while engaging with diverse criteria."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 8 figures; Accepted by CHI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05742","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:55:57Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:55:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-04T03:41:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05052","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05052","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05052","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiliang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Weiliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinjun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Jinjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Ben-Levi","name":"Ben-Levi, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhou","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junfeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Junfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Proactive defense against LLM Jailbreak"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:32:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-02T19:01:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-04T01:05:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The proliferation of powerful large language models (LLMs) has necessitated robust safety alignment, yet these models remain vulnerable to evolving adversarial attacks, including multi-turn jailbreaks that iteratively search for successful queries. Current defenses, which are primarily reactive and static, often fail to handle these iterative attacks. In this paper, we introduce ProAct, a novel proactive defense framework designed to disrupt and mislead these iterative search jailbreak methods. Our core idea is to intentionally mislead these jailbreak methods into thinking that the model has been jailbroken with \"spurious responses\". These misleading responses provide false signals to the attacker's internal optimization loop, causing the adversarial search to terminate prematurely and effectively jailbreaking the jailbreak. By conducting extensive experiments across state-of-the-art LLMs, jailbreaking frameworks, and safety benchmarks, we demonstrate that our method consistently and significantly reduces attack success rates by up to 94% without affecting utility. When combined with other defense fraeworks, it further reduces the latest attack strategies' success rate to 0%. ProActrepresents an orthogonal defense strategy that serves as an additional guardrail to enhance LLM safety against the most effective jailbreaking attacks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05052","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:04:18Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:04:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-04T03:41:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05609","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05609","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05609","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junwen","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Junwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peilin","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Peilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keiji","familyName":"Yanai","name":"Yanai, Keiji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HOI-R1: Exploring the Potential of Multimodal Large Language Models for Human-Object Interaction Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T06:16:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:27:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-01T03:07:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:59:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent human-object interaction detection (HOID) methods highly require prior knowledge from vision-language models (VLMs) to enhance the interaction recognition capabilities. The training strategies and model architectures for connecting the knowledge from VLMs to the HOI instance representations from the object detector are challenging, and the whole framework is complex for further development or application. On the other hand, the inherent reasoning abilities of multimodal large language models (MLLMs) on human-object interaction detection are under-explored. Inspired by the recent success of training MLLMs with reinforcement learning (RL) methods, we propose HOI-R1 and first explore the potential of the language model on the HOID task without any additional detection modules. We introduce an HOI reasoning process and HOID reward functions to solve the HOID task by pure text. Experiments on HICO-DET across multiple open-source MLLMs, including the Qwen-VL family (Qwen2.5-VL and Qwen3-VL) and Rex-Omni, show consistent improvements. Especially, HOI-R1 boosts Qwen2.5-VL-3B 2$\\times$ accuracy with great generalization ability. The source code is available at https://github.com/cjw2021/HOI-R1."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05609","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:52Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:41:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05176","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05176","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05176","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoxiong","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Shaoxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiwen","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Peiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinglin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yueqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuyi","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Chuyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyuan","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Boyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PatternKV: Flattening KV Representation Expands Quantization Headroom"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T12:09:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-31T06:41:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:30:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"KV cache in autoregressive LLMs eliminates redundant recomputation but has emerged as the dominant memory and bandwidth bottleneck during inference, notably with long contexts and test-time scaling. KV quantization is a key lever for reducing cache cost, but accuracy drops sharply as the native KV distribution lacks flatness and thus maintains a wide quantization range. Prior work focuses on isolating outliers, which caps their error but fails to flatten the overall distribution, leaving performance fragile under low-bit settings. In this work, we show that the K cache maintains a stable, context-evolving structure, while the V cache carries latent semantic regularities, with both contributing to the organization of vectors into shared patterns. Building on these insights, we propose PatternKV, a pattern-aligned residual quantization scheme. It mines representative pattern vectors online, aligns each KV vector to its nearest pattern, and quantizes only the residual. This reshaping of the KV distribution flattens the quantization target and narrows its range, thereby improving the fidelity of low-bit KV quantization. Across long-context and test-time scaling settings on multiple backbones, PatternKV delivers consistent 2-bit gains, with a 0.08% average 4-bit drop relative to FP16, improves test-time scaling accuracy by 10% on average, and raises throughput by 1.5x while supporting 1.25x larger batches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05176","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:50Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:41:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06213","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06213","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06213","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Albert","familyName":"Catalan-Tatjer","name":"Catalan-Tatjer, Albert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niccolò","familyName":"Ajroldi","name":"Ajroldi, Niccolò","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Geiping","name":"Geiping, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Training Dynamics Impact Post-Training Quantization Robustness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:59:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T01:07:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T09:50:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:40:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While post-training quantization is widely adopted for efficient deployment of large language models, the mechanisms underlying quantization robustness remain unclear. We conduct a comprehensive analysis of quantization degradation across open-source language model training trajectories up to 32B parameters and 15T training tokens to accurately assess the relationship between training dynamics and quantization performance. Our key finding is that quantization errors in large-scale training runs are driven by a complex interplay between learning rate and other training hyperparameters. Specifically, once learning rates decay, validation loss and quantization error diverge, largely independent of training data scale. To investigate interventions on the training dynamics and identify specific configurations that can modulate quantization robustness favorably, we train our own models in controlled experiments up to 100B tokens. Our results challenge the assumption that increasing dataset scale inherently compromises quantization effectiveness, demonstrating instead that strategic training hyperparameter interventions can improve quantization quality at scale."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06213","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:07:02Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:07:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:25:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05758","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05758","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05758","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haoxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanlei","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Hanlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leyuan","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Leyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taihao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Taihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EMORL-TTS: Reinforcement Learning for Fine-Grained Emotion Control in LLM-based TTS"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:24:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:39:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-24T06:22:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T01:17:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent LLM-based TTS systems achieve strong quality and zero-shot ability, but lack fine-grained emotional control due to their reliance on discrete speech tokens. Existing approaches either limit emotions to categorical labels or cannot generalize to LLM-based architectures. We propose EMORL-TTS (Fine-grained Emotion-controllable TTS with Reinforcement Learning), a framework that unifies global intensity control in the VAD space with local emphasis regulation. Our method combines supervised fine-tuning with reinforcement learning guided by task-specific rewards for emotion category, intensity, and emphasis. Moreover, we further investigate how emphasis placement modulates fine-grained emotion intensity. Experiments show that EMORL-TTS improves emotion accuracy, intensity differentiation, and emphasis clarity, while preserving synthesis quality comparable to strong LLM-based baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICASSP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05758","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:56:19Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:56:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:35:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05749","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05749","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05749","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haoxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanlei","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Hanlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leyuan","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Leyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taihao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Taihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MSF-SER: Enriching Acoustic Modeling with Multi-Granularity Semantics for Speech Emotion Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:11:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:38:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-24T06:18:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T01:17:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Continuous dimensional speech emotion recognition captures affective variation along valence, arousal, and dominance, providing finer-grained representations than categorical approaches. Yet most multimodal methods rely solely on global transcripts, leading to two limitations: (1) all words are treated equally, overlooking that emphasis on different parts of a sentence can shift emotional meaning; (2) only surface lexical content is represented, lacking higher-level interpretive cues. To overcome these issues, we propose MSF-SER (Multi-granularity Semantic Fusion for Speech Emotion Recognition), which augments acoustic features with three complementary levels of textual semantics--Local Emphasized Semantics (LES), Global Semantics (GS), and Extended Semantics (ES). These are integrated via an intra-modal gated fusion and a cross-modal FiLM-modulated lightweight Mixture-of-Experts (FM-MOE). Experiments on MSP-Podcast and IEMOCAP show that MSF-SER consistently improves dimensional prediction, demonstrating the effectiveness of enriched semantic fusion for SER."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICASSP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05749","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:56:06Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:56:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:35:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06092","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06092","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06092","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nyal","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Nyal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthieu","familyName":"Bou","name":"Bou, Matthieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arjun","familyName":"Jagota","name":"Jagota, Arjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satyapriya","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Satyapriya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sonali","familyName":"Parbhoo","name":"Parbhoo, Sonali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning from Failures: Understanding LLM Alignment through Failure-Aware Inverse RL"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:20:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:57:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-17T10:13:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T01:25:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) aligns Large Language Models (LLMs) with human preferences, yet the underlying reward signals they internalize remain hidden, posing a critical challenge for interpretability and safety. Existing approaches attempt to extract these latent incentives using Inverse Reinforcement Learning (IRL), but treat all preference pairs equally, often overlooking the most informative signals: those examples the extracted reward model misclassifies or assigns nearly equal scores, which we term \\emph{failures}. We introduce a novel \\emph{failure-aware} IRL algorithm that focuses on misclassified or difficult examples to recover the latent rewards defining model behaviors. By learning from these failures, our failure-aware IRL extracts reward functions that better reflect the true objectives behind RLHF. We demonstrate that failure-aware IRL outperforms existing IRL baselines across multiple metrics when applied to LLM detoxification, without requiring external classifiers or supervision. Crucially, failure-aware IRL yields rewards that better capture the true incentives learned during RLHF, enabling more effective re-RLHF training than standard IRL. This establishes failure-aware IRL as a robust, scalable method for auditing model alignment and reducing ambiguity in the IRL process."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06092","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:12Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:06:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05484","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05484","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05484","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhejay","familyName":"Murali","name":"Murali, Abhejay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saleh","familyName":"Afroogh","name":"Afroogh, Saleh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Atkinson","name":"Atkinson, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amit","familyName":"Dhurandhar","name":"Dhurandhar, Amit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junfeng","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Junfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating LLM Safety Across Child Development Stages: A Simulated Agent Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T01:01:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:16:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-17T18:37:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T01:35:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current safety alignment for Large Language Models (LLMs) implicitly optimizes for a \"modal adult user,\" leaving models vulnerable to distributional shifts in user cognition. We present ChildSafe, a benchmark that quantifies alignment robustness under cognitive shifts corresponding to four developmental stages. Unlike static persona-based evaluations, we introduce a parametric cognitive simulation approach, formalizing developmental stages as hyperparameter constraints (e.g., volatility, context horizon) to generate out-of-distribution interaction traces. We validate these agents against ground-truth human linguistic data (CHILDES) and deploy them across 1,200 multi-turn interactions. Our results reveal a systematic alignment generalization gap: state-of-the-art models exhibit up to 11.5% performance degradation when interacting with early-childhood agents compared to standard baselines. We provide the research community with the validated agent artifacts and evaluation protocols to facilitate robust alignment testing against non-adversarial, cognitively diverse populations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05484","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:57Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:06:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05134","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05134","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05134","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ancheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingpeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jingpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiehui","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jiehui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengyu","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Hengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yukun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longze","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Longze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmadreza","familyName":"Argha","name":"Argha, Ahmadreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Alinejad-Rokny","name":"Alinejad-Rokny, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghuan","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Minghuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujun","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structuring Reasoning for Complex Rules Beyond Flat Representations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T04:10:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-20T12:09:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T03:15:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) face significant challenges when processing complex rule systems, as they typically treat interdependent rules as unstructured textual data rather than as logically organized frameworks. This limitation results in reasoning divergence, where models often overlook critical rule dependencies essential for accurate interpretation. Although existing approaches such as Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning have shown promise, they lack systematic methodologies for structured rule processing and are particularly susceptible to error propagation through sequential reasoning chains. To address these limitations, we propose the Dynamic Adjudication Template (DAT), a novel framework inspired by expert human reasoning processes. DAT structures the inference mechanism into three methodical stages: qualitative analysis, evidence gathering, and adjudication. During the qualitative analysis phase, the model comprehensively evaluates the contextual landscape. The subsequent evidence gathering phase involves the targeted extraction of pertinent information based on predefined template elements ([placeholder]), followed by systematic verification against applicable rules. Finally, in the adjudication phase, the model synthesizes these validated components to formulate a comprehensive judgment. Empirical results demonstrate that DAT consistently outperforms conventional CoT approaches in complex rule-based tasks. Notably, DAT enables smaller language models to match, and in some cases exceed, the performance of significantly larger LLMs, highlighting its efficiency and effectiveness in managing intricate rule systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05134","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:52Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:06:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05014","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05014","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05014","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanming","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Xuanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianpeng","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Jianpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong-you","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hong-you","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satya Narayan","familyName":"Shukla","name":"Shukla, Satya Narayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhijeet","familyName":"Awasthi","name":"Awasthi, Abhijeet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xichen","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Xichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaitanya","familyName":"Ahuja","name":"Ahuja, Chaitanya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shlok Kumar","familyName":"Mishra","name":"Mishra, Shlok Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonghuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yonghuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ser-Nam","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Ser-Nam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aashu","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Aashu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangjun","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Xiangjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Think Then Embed: Generative Context Improves Multimodal Embedding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:53:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:45:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T00:57:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:14:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T23:44:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:13:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-19T16:13:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-21T02:34:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"There is a growing interest in Universal Multimodal Embeddings (UME), where models are required to generate task-specific representations. While recent studies show that Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) perform well on such tasks, they treat MLLMs solely as encoders, overlooking their generative capacity. However, such an encoding paradigm becomes less effective as instructions become more complex and require compositional reasoning. Inspired by the proven effectiveness of chain-of-thought reasoning, we propose a general Think-Then-Embed (TTE) framework for UME, composed of a reasoner and an embedder. The reasoner MLLM first generates reasoning traces that explain complex queries, followed by an embedder that produces representations conditioned on both the original query and the intermediate reasoning. This explicit reasoning step enables more nuanced understanding of complex multimodal instructions. Our contributions are threefold. First, by leveraging a powerful MLLM reasoner, we achieve state-of-the-art performance on the MMEB-V2 benchmark, surpassing proprietary models trained on massive in-house datasets. Second, to reduce the dependency on large MLLM reasoners, we finetune a smaller MLLM reasoner using high-quality embedding-centric reasoning traces, achieving the best performance among open-source models with a 7% absolute gain over recently proposed models. Third, we investigate strategies for integrating the reasoner and embedder into a unified model for improved efficiency without sacrificing performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05014","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:02:43Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:02:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:05:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05611","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05611","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05611","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Chieh","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wei-Chieh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cornelia","familyName":"Caragea","name":"Caragea, Cornelia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MADIAVE: Multi-Agent Debate for Implicit Attribute Value Extraction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T06:27:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:27:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-15T19:56:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-19T01:04:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Implicit Attribute Value Extraction (AVE) is essential for accurately representing products in e-commerce, as it infers latent attributes from multimodal data. Despite advances in multimodal large language models (MLLMs), implicit AVE remains challenging due to the complexity of multidimensional data and gaps in vision-text understanding. In this work, we introduce MADIAVE, a multi-agent debate framework that employs multiple MLLM agents to iteratively refine inferences. Through a series of debate rounds, agents verify and update each other's responses, thereby improving inference performance and robustness. Experiments on the ImplicitAVE dataset demonstrate that even a few rounds of debate significantly boost accuracy, especially for attributes with initially low performance. We systematically evaluate various debate configurations, including identical or different MLLM agents, and analyze how debate rounds affect convergence dynamics. Our findings highlight the potential of multi-agent debate strategies to address the limitations of single-agent approaches and offer a scalable solution for implicit AVE in multimodal e-commerce."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EACL 2026 (Findings)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05611","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:54Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-19T02:45:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05465","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05465","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05465","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aman","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Aman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Denny","familyName":"O'Shea","name":"O'Shea, Denny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fazl","familyName":"Barez","name":"Barez, Fazl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VAL-Bench: Belief Consistency as a measure for Value Alignment in Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T23:55:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:15:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:35:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:16:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-14T19:30:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-16T01:02:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly being used for tasks where outputs shape human decisions, so it is critical to verify that their responses consistently reflect desired human values. Humans, as individuals or groups, don't agree on a universal set of values, which makes evaluating value alignment difficult. Existing benchmarks often use hypothetical or commonsensical situations, which don't capture the complexity and ambiguity of real-life debates. We introduce the Value ALignment Benchmark (VAL-Bench), which measures the consistency in language model belief expressions in response to real-life value-laden prompts. VAL-Bench consists of 115K pairs of prompts designed to elicit opposing stances on a controversial issue, extracted from Wikipedia. We use an LLM-as-a-judge, validated against human annotations, to evaluate if the pair of responses consistently expresses either a neutral or a specific stance on the issue. Applied across leading open- and closed-source models, the benchmark shows considerable variation in consistency rates (ranging from ~10% to ~80%), with Claude models the only ones to achieve high levels of consistency. Lack of consistency in this manner risks epistemic harm by making user beliefs dependent on how questions are framed rather than on underlying evidence, and undermines LLM reliability in trust-critical applications. Therefore, we stress the importance of research towards training belief consistency in modern LLMs. By providing a scalable, reproducible benchmark, VAL-Bench enables systematic measurement of necessary conditions for value alignment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05465","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:31Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-16T03:11:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05774","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05774","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05774","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weichun","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Weichun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Minghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanting","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wanting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Langchen","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Langchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuqi","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Fuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feifei","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Feifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ConstraintLLM: A Neuro-Symbolic Framework for Industrial-Level Constraint Programming"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:43:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:39:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.809","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Constraint programming (CP) is a crucial technology for solving real-world constraint optimization problems (COPs), with the advantages of rich modeling semantics and high solving efficiency. Using large language models (LLMs) to generate formal modeling automatically for COPs is becoming a promising approach, which aims to build trustworthy neuro-symbolic AI with the help of symbolic solvers. However, CP has received less attention compared to works based on operations research (OR) models. We introduce ConstraintLLM, the first LLM specifically designed for CP modeling, which is trained on an open-source LLM with multi-instruction supervised fine-tuning. We propose the Constraint-Aware Retrieval Module (CARM) to increase the in-context learning capabilities, which is integrated in a Tree-of-Thoughts (ToT) framework with guided self-correction mechanism. Moreover, we construct and release IndusCP, the first industrial-level benchmark for CP modeling, which contains 140 challenging tasks from various domains. Our experiments demonstrate that ConstraintLLM achieves state-of-the-art solving accuracy across multiple benchmarks and outperforms the baselines by 2x on the new IndusCP benchmark. Code and data are available at: https://github.com/william4s/ConstraintLLM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the 2025 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP 2025), Main Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05774","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:56:41Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:56:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:26:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06187","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06187","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06187","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Griffin","familyName":"Pitts","name":"Pitts, Griffin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aum","familyName":"Pandya","name":"Pandya, Aum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Darsh","familyName":"Rank","name":"Rank, Darsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tirth","familyName":"Bhatt","name":"Bhatt, Tirth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muntasir","familyName":"Hoq","name":"Hoq, Muntasir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bita","familyName":"Akram","name":"Akram, Bita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automated Program Repair of Uncompilable Student Code"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:46:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:02:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-28T21:25:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:07:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-23T07:41:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-24T01:25:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3770761.3777323","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A significant portion of student programming submissions in CS1 learning environments are uncompilable, limiting their use in student modeling and downstream knowledge tracing. Traditional modeling pipelines often exclude these cases, discarding observations of student learning. This study investigates automated program repair as a strategy to recover uncompilable code while preserving students' structural intent for use in student modeling. Within this framework, we assess large language models (LLMs) as repair agents under high- and low-context prompting conditions. Repairs were evaluated for compilability, edit distance, and preservation of students' original structure and logic. While all models produced compilable repairs, they differed in how well they preserve students' control flow and code structure, affecting their pedagogical utility. By recovering uncompilable submissions, this work enables richer and more comprehensive analyses of learners' coding processes and development over time."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In Proceedings of the 57th ACM Technical Symposium on Computer Science Education V.2 (SIGCSE TS 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06187","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:24Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-24T02:56:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04944","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04944","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04944","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jerry Yao-Chieh","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jerry Yao-Chieh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiwen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"ElSheikh","name":"ElSheikh, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weimin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Weimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Structured State-Space Duality"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:46:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:42:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-23T05:51:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-24T01:20:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Structured State-Space Duality (SSD) [Dao \u0026amp; Gu, ICML 2024] is an equivalence between a simple Structured State-Space Model (SSM) and a masked attention mechanism. In particular, a state-space model with a scalar-times-identity state matrix is equivalent to a masked self-attention with a $1$-semiseparable causal mask. Consequently, the same sequence transformation (model) has two algorithmic realizations: as a linear-time $O(T)$ recurrence or as a quadratic-time $O(T^2)$ attention. In this note, we formalize and generalize this duality: (i) we extend SSD from the scalar-identity case to general diagonal SSMs (diagonal state matrices); (ii) we show that these diagonal SSMs match the scalar case's training complexity lower bounds while supporting richer dynamics; (iii) we establish a necessary and sufficient condition under which an SSM is equivalent to $1$-semiseparable masked attention; and (iv) we show that such duality fails to extend to standard softmax attention due to rank explosion. Together, these results tighten bridge between recurrent SSMs and Transformers, and widen the design space for expressive yet efficient sequence models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v2 fixed typos and added numerical results (Appendix B)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04944","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:59:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:59:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-24T02:56:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06215","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06215","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06215","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayush","familyName":"Shrivastava","name":"Shrivastava, Ayush","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Connelly","familyName":"Barnes","name":"Barnes, Connelly","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuaner","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuaner","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingzhi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lingzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Owens","name":"Owens, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sohrab","familyName":"Amirghodsi","name":"Amirghodsi, Sohrab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eli","familyName":"Shechtman","name":"Shechtman, Eli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fine-grained Defocus Blur Control for Generative Image Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:59:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-12T05:39:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:23:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current text-to-image diffusion models excel at generating diverse, high-quality images, yet they struggle to incorporate fine-grained camera metadata such as precise aperture settings. In this work, we introduce a novel text-to-image diffusion framework that leverages camera metadata, or EXIF data, which is often embedded in image files, with an emphasis on generating controllable lens blur. Our method mimics the physical image formation process by first generating an all-in-focus image, estimating its monocular depth, predicting a plausible focus distance with a novel focus distance transformer, and then forming a defocused image with an existing differentiable lens blur model. Gradients flow backwards through this whole process, allowing us to learn without explicit supervision to generate defocus effects based on content elements and the provided EXIF data. At inference time, this enables precise interactive user control over defocus effects while preserving scene contents, which is not achievable with existing diffusion models. Experimental results demonstrate that our model enables superior fine-grained control without altering the depicted scene."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project link: https://www.ayshrv.com/defocus-blur-gen"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06215","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:07:05Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:07:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-15T02:58:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05526","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05526","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05526","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ziyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Junyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiran","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Peiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Provably Mitigating Corruption, Overoptimization, and Verbosity Simultaneously in Offline and Online RLHF/DPO Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T02:32:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:19:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-09T17:10:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-10T02:00:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) and direct preference optimization (DPO) are important techniques to align large language models (LLM) with human preference. However, the quality of RLHF and DPO training is seriously compromised by \\textit{\\textbf{C}orrupted} preference, reward \\textit{\\textbf{O}veroptimization}, and bias towards \\textit{\\textbf{V}erbosity}. To our knowledge, most existing works tackle only one of these important issues, and the few other works require much computation to estimate multiple reward models and lack theoretical guarantee of generalization ability. In this work, we propose RLHF-\\textbf{COV} and DPO-\\textbf{COV} algorithms that can simultaneously mitigate these three issues, in both offline and online settings. This ability is theoretically demonstrated by obtaining length-regularized generalization error rates for our DPO-COV algorithms trained on corrupted data, which match the best-known rates for simpler cases with clean data and without length regularization. Moreover, our DPO-COV algorithm is simple to implement without reward estimation, and is proved to be equivalent to our RLHF-COV algorithm, which directly implies the equivalence between the vanilla RLHF and DPO algorithms. Experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of our DPO-COV algorithms under both offline and online settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Edited a few incorrect numbers in Tables 2 and 3"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05526","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:50:55Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:50:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-10T03:17:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04996","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04996","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04996","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenlu","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Chenlu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baohao","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Baohao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanze","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Hanze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinxing","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xinxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christof","familyName":"Monz","name":"Monz, Christof","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiang","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Jiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reinforce-Ada: An Adaptive Sampling Framework under Non-linear RL Objectives"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:34:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:45:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:57:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:16:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-05T17:41:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:47:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) for large language model reasoning is frequently hindered by signal loss, a phenomenon where standard uniform sampling with small group sizes fails to uncover informative learning signals for difficult prompts. We demonstrate that this collapse is a statistical artifact of undersampling rather than an inherent model limitation. To address this systematically, we introduce a theoretical framework based on optimizing a non-linear RL objective (e.g., log-likelihood). We show that this objective naturally induces a weighted gradient estimator that prioritizes difficult prompts, which can be robustly realized through adaptive sampling. Guided by this framework, we propose Reinforce-Ada, a family of algorithms that dynamically allocates inference budgets based on prompt difficulty, effectively scaling up RL compute to where it is needed most. Unlike passive filtering methods that discard low-signal prompts, Reinforce-Ada actively invests compute to recover them. We introduce two efficient realizations: an estimation-based approach and a model-free sequential sampling approach. Extensive experiments across multiple benchmarks show that Reinforce-Ada significantly outperforms uniform baselines like GRPO, recovering lost signals and accelerating convergence by up to $2\\times$ while maintaining the same total inference budget. Code is available at https://github.com/RLHFlow/Reinforce-Ada."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04996","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:01:59Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:02:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-08T02:47:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06108","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06108","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06108","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prateek","familyName":"Humane","name":"Humane, Prateek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paolo","familyName":"Cudrano","name":"Cudrano, Paolo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel Z.","familyName":"Kaplan","name":"Kaplan, Daniel Z.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo","familyName":"Matteucci","name":"Matteucci, Matteo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Supriyo","familyName":"Chakraborty","name":"Chakraborty, Supriyo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irina","familyName":"Rish","name":"Rish, Irina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Influence Functions for Efficient Data Selection in Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:40:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:58:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-01T16:49:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T02:44:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fine-tuning large language models (LLMs) on chain-of-thought (CoT) data shows that a small amount of high-quality data can outperform massive datasets. Yet, what constitutes \"quality\" remains ill-defined. Existing reasoning methods rely on indirect heuristics such as problem difficulty or trace length, while instruction-tuning has explored a broader range of automated selection strategies, but rarely in the context of reasoning. We propose to define reasoning data quality using influence functions, which measure the causal effect of individual CoT examples on downstream accuracy, and introduce influence-based pruning, which consistently outperforms perplexity and embedding-based baselines on math reasoning within a model family."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"4 pages, 2 figures; added link to codebase"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06108","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:33Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:47:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05140","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05140","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05140","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Armin","familyName":"Gerami","name":"Gerami, Armin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramani","familyName":"Duraiswami","name":"Duraiswami, Ramani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Auditing Algorithmic Bias in Transformer-Based Trading"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T21:20:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-30T23:39:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T02:01:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer models have become increasingly popular in financial applications, yet their potential risk making and biases remain under-explored. The purpose of this work is to audit the reliance of the model on volatile data for decision-making, and quantify how the frequency of price movements affects the model's prediction confidence. We employ a transformer model for prediction, and introduce a metric based on Partial Information Decomposition (PID) to measure the influence of each asset on the model's decision making. Our analysis reveals two key observations: first, the model disregards data volatility entirely, and second, it is biased toward data with lower-frequency price movements."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05140","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:00Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:47:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05034","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05034","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05034","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yolo Y.","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yolo Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Bi","name":"Bi, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pinxin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Pinxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhangyun","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Zhangyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianxiang","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Qianxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiani","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjia","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Junjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunzhong","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yunzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Susan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhi","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhua","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Junhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia-Xing","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Jia-Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bozheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bozheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daiqing","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Daiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyun","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Ziyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Vosoughi","name":"Vosoughi, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luchuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Luchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeliang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zeliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daiki","familyName":"Shimada","name":"Shimada, Daiki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiebo","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Jiebo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenliang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chenliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Video-LMM Post-Training: A Deep Dive into Video Reasoning with Large Multimodal Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:10:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:46:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T16:08:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:57:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T17:59:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:15:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-13T16:09:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-14T02:07:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-28T18:02:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:03:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-11-25T03:36:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-11-26T01:24:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video understanding represents the most challenging frontier in computer vision, requiring models to reason about complex spatiotemporal relationships, long-term dependencies, and multimodal evidence. The recent emergence of Video-Large Multimodal Models (Video-LMMs), which integrate visual encoders with powerful decoder-based language models, has demonstrated remarkable capabilities in video understanding tasks. However, the critical phase that transforms these models from basic perception systems into sophisticated reasoning engines, post-training, remains fragmented across the literature. This survey provides the first comprehensive examination of post-training methodologies for Video-LMMs, encompassing three fundamental pillars: supervised fine-tuning (SFT) with chain-of-thought, reinforcement learning (RL) from verifiable objectives, and test-time scaling (TTS) through enhanced inference computation. We present a structured taxonomy that clarifies the roles, interconnections, and video-specific adaptations of these techniques, addressing unique challenges such as temporal localization, spatiotemporal grounding, long video efficiency, and multimodal evidence integration. Through systematic analysis of representative methods, we synthesize key design principles, insights, and evaluation protocols while identifying critical open challenges in reward design, scalability, and cost-performance optimization. We further curate essential benchmarks, datasets, and metrics to facilitate rigorous assessment of post-training effectiveness. This survey aims to provide researchers and practitioners with a unified framework for advancing Video-LMM capabilities. Additional resources and updates are maintained at: https://github.com/yunlong10/Awesome-Video-LMM-Post-Training"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Version v1.1"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05034","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:03:34Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:03:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-26T03:24:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05814","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05814","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05814","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-Hsin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yi-Hsin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mårten","familyName":"Sjöström","name":"Sjöström, Mårten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Knorr","name":"Knorr, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Sikora","name":"Sikora, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rasterized Steered Mixture of Experts for Efficient 2D Image Regression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T11:32:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:41:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-18T13:46:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-19T01:50:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Steered Mixture of Experts regression framework has demonstrated strong performance in image reconstruction, compression, denoising, and super-resolution. However, its high computational cost limits practical applications. This work introduces a rasterization-based optimization strategy that combines the efficiency of rasterized Gaussian kernel rendering with the edge-aware gating mechanism of the Steered Mixture of Experts. The proposed method is designed to accelerate two-dimensional image regression while maintaining the model's inherent sparsity and reconstruction quality. By replacing global iterative optimization with a rasterized formulation, the method achieves significantly faster parameter updates and more memory-efficient model representations. In addition, the proposed framework supports applications such as native super-resolution and image denoising, which are not directly achievable with standard rasterized Gaussian kernel approaches. The combination of fast rasterized optimization with the edge-aware structure of the Steered Mixture of Experts provides a new balance between computational efficiency and reconstruction fidelity for two-dimensional image processing tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05814","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:57:37Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:57:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-19T03:17:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06190","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06190","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06190","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxiao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chenxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cai","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Cai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Wipf","name":"Wipf, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Powerful Ways to Generate: Autoregression, Diffusion, and Beyond"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:49:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:02:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-17T03:58:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T02:22:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion language models have recently emerged as a competitive alternative to autoregressive language models. Beyond next-token generation, they are more efficient and flexible by enabling parallel and any-order token generation. However, despite empirical successes, their computational power and fundamental limitations remain poorly understood. In this paper, we formally study whether non-autoregressive generation in Masked Diffusion Models (MDM) enables solving problems beyond the reach of Auto-Regressive Models (ARM). Our results show that MDM with sufficiently large context length is computationally universal with decoding steps matching the optimal parallel time complexity in PRAM. However, when controlling for other factors, MDM's flexibility to generate in any-order does not expand what ARM can already solve. To address this, we propose a new form of generation called any-process generation, which extends MDM with capabilities to remask, insert and delete tokens, allowing self-correction, length-variable editing, and adaptive parallelism. Theoretically and empirically, we demonstrate these capabilities enable scalability to significantly harder reasoning problems that are otherwise intractable for ARM and vanilla MDM. Additionally, they prove essential for generation tasks where objects naturally evolve through non-sequential processes, crucial for extending current LLMs beyond natural language to domains such as coding and science."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06190","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:29Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:16:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06067","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06067","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06067","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Python","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Python","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke Tenyi","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Luke Tenyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun-Yun","familyName":"Tsai","name":"Tsai, Yun-Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Penghui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Penghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junfeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Junfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning under Vision: Understanding Visual-Spatial Cognition in Vision-Language Models for CAPTCHA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:56:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-16T00:34:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:48:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"CAPTCHA, originally designed to distinguish humans from robots, has evolved into a real-world benchmark for assessing the spatial reasoning capabilities of vision-language models. In this work, we first show that step-by-step reasoning is crucial for vision-language models (VLMs) to solve CAPTCHAs, which represent high-difficulty spatial reasoning tasks, and that current commercial vision-language models still struggle with such reasoning. In particular, we observe that most commercial VLMs (e.g., Gemini, Claude, GPT, etc.) fail to effectively solve CAPTCHAs and thus achieve low accuracy (around 21.9 percent). However, our findings indicate that requiring the model to perform step-by-step reasoning before generating the final coordinates can significantly enhance its solving accuracy, underscoring the severity of the gap. To systematically study this issue, we introduce CAPTCHA-X, the first real-world CAPTCHA benchmark with reasoning, covering seven categories of CAPTCHAs (such as Gobang, hCaptcha, etc.) with step-by-step action solutions and grounding annotations. We further define five reasoning-oriented metrics that enable a comprehensive evaluation of models reasoning capabilities. To validate the effectiveness of reasoning, we also propose a general agentic VLM-based framework that incorporates the models inherent reasoning abilities. Our method achieves state-of-the-art performance across five high-difficulty CAPTCHA types, with an average solving accuracy of 83.9 percent, substantially surpassing existing baselines. These results reveal the limitations of current models and highlight the importance of reasoning in advancing visual-spatial challenges in the future."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14pages, 11figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06067","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:36Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:15:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05162","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05162","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05162","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gerd","familyName":"Kortemeyer","name":"Kortemeyer, Gerd","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Caspar","name":"Caspar, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daria","familyName":"Horica","name":"Horica, Daria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Artificial-Intelligence Grading Assistance for Handwritten Components of a Calculus Exam"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T15:07:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-13T10:55:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-14T01:37:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate whether contemporary multimodal LLMs can assist with grading open-ended calculus at scale without eroding validity. In a large first-year exam, students' handwritten work was graded by GPT-5 against the same rubric used by teaching assistants (TAs), with fractional credit permitted; TA rubric decisions served as ground truth. We calibrated a human-in-the-loop filter that combines a partial-credit threshold with an Item Response Theory (2PL) risk measure based on the deviation between the AI score and the model-expected score for each student-item. Unfiltered AI-TA agreement was moderate, adequate for low-stakes feedback but not for high-stakes use. Confidence filtering made the workload-quality trade-off explicit: under stricter settings, AI delivered human-level accuracy, but also left roughly 70% of the items to be graded by humans. Psychometric patterns were constrained by low stakes on the open-ended portion, a small set of rubric checkpoints, and occasional misalignment between designated answer regions and where work appeared. Practical adjustments such as slightly higher weight and protected time, a few rubric-visible substeps, stronger spatial anchoring should raise ceiling performance. Overall, calibrated confidence and conservative routing enable AI to reliably handle a sizable subset of routine cases while reserving expert judgment for ambiguous or pedagogically rich responses."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05162","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:30Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-14T03:11:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04983","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04983","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04983","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khalid Mehtab","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Khalid Mehtab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anagha","familyName":"Kulkarni","name":"Kulkarni, Anagha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AWARE, Beyond Sentence Boundaries: A Contextual Transformer Framework for Identifying Cultural Capital in STEM Narratives"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:19:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:44:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T05:08:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:25:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T21:48:02Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v3; The authors are withdrawing this version to correct issues identified in the experimental design and analysis. A revised and validated version will be submitted after further review"},{"date":"2025-11-05T01:08:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Identifying cultural capital (CC) themes in student reflections can offer valuable insights that help foster equitable learning environments in classrooms. However, themes such as aspirational goals or family support are often woven into narratives, rather than appearing as direct keywords. This makes them difficult to detect for standard NLP models that process sentences in isolation. The core challenge stems from a lack of awareness, as standard models are pre-trained on general corpora, leaving them blind to the domain-specific language and narrative context inherent to the data. To address this, we introduce AWARE, a framework that systematically attempts to improve a transformer model's awareness for this nuanced task. AWARE has three core components: 1) Domain Awareness, adapting the model's vocabulary to the linguistic style of student reflections; 2) Context Awareness, generating sentence embeddings that are aware of the full essay context; and 3) Class Overlap Awareness, employing a multi-label strategy to recognize the coexistence of themes in a single sentence. Our results show that by making the model explicitly aware of the properties of the input, AWARE outperforms a strong baseline by 2.1 percentage points in Macro-F1 and shows considerable improvements across all themes. This work provides a robust and generalizable methodology for any text classification task in which meaning depends on the context of the narrative."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The authors are withdrawing this version to correct issues identified in the experimental design and analysis. A revised and validated version will be submitted after further review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04983","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:01:27Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:01:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-05T03:12:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05702","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05702","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05702","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabrizio","familyName":"Dimino","name":"Dimino, Fabrizio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krati","familyName":"Saxena","name":"Saxena, Krati","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhaskarjit","familyName":"Sarmah","name":"Sarmah, Bhaskarjit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Pasquali","name":"Pasquali, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uncovering Representation Bias for Investment Decisions in Open-Source Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Finance (q-fin.CP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T09:10:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:34:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-03T01:00:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:09:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models are increasingly adopted in financial applications to support investment workflows. However, prior studies have seldom examined how these models reflect biases related to firm size, sector, or financial characteristics, which can significantly impact decision-making. This paper addresses this gap by focusing on representation bias in open-source Qwen models. We propose a balanced round-robin prompting method over approximately 150 U.S. equities, applying constrained decoding and token-logit aggregation to derive firm-level confidence scores across financial contexts. Using statistical tests and variance analysis, we find that firm size and valuation consistently increase model confidence, while risk factors tend to decrease it. Confidence varies significantly across sectors, with the Technology sector showing the greatest variability. When models are prompted for specific financial categories, their confidence rankings best align with fundamental data, moderately with technical signals, and least with growth indicators. These results highlight representation bias in Qwen models and motivate sector-aware calibration and category-conditioned evaluation protocols for safe and fair financial LLM deployment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05702","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:55:01Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:55:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-04T04:57:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05024","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05024","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05024","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nevan","familyName":"Wichers","name":"Wichers, Nevan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aram","familyName":"Ebtekar","name":"Ebtekar, Aram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ariana","familyName":"Azarbal","name":"Azarbal, Ariana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Gillioz","name":"Gillioz, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christine","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Christine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emil","familyName":"Ryd","name":"Ryd, Emil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neil","familyName":"Rathi","name":"Rathi, Neil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Sleight","name":"Sleight, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Mallen","name":"Mallen, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabien","familyName":"Roger","name":"Roger, Fabien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Marks","name":"Marks, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inoculation Prompting: Instructing LLMs to misbehave at train-time improves test-time alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:02:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:46:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T03:13:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:20:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-27T21:05:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:11:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are sometimes trained with imperfect oversight signals, leading to undesired behaviors such as reward hacking and sycophancy. Improving oversight quality can be expensive or infeasible, motivating methods that improve learned behavior despite an imperfect training signal. We introduce Inoculation Prompting (IP), a simple but counterintuitive technique that prevents learning of an undesired behavior by modifying training prompts to explicitly request it. For example, to inoculate against reward hacking, we modify the prompts used in supervised fine-tuning to request code that only works on provided test cases but fails on other inputs. Across four settings we find that IP reduces the learning of undesired behavior without substantially reducing the learning of desired capabilities. We also show that prompts which more strongly elicit the undesired behavior prior to fine-tuning more effectively inoculate against the behavior when used during training; this serves as a heuristic to identify promising inoculation prompts. Overall, IP is a simple yet effective way to control how models generalize from fine-tuning, preventing learning of undesired behaviors without substantially disrupting desired capabilities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v2 Updates references. v3 Updates references; Adds IFEval results; Improves appendix readability; Adds author contributions"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05024","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:03:10Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:03:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-29T02:23:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06186","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06186","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06186","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunyu","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Chunyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry Peng","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Henry Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangning","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yangning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yankai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yankai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yibo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangxin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Fangxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wooseong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Wooseong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Bowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinni","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xinni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dianzhi","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Dianzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanchen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoang H","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Hoang H","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jizhou","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenzhe","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Wenzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chin-Yuan","familyName":"Yeh","name":"Yeh, Chin-Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Panpan","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Panpan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liancheng","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Liancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinhu","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Jinhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Chieh","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wei-Chieh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyao","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Zhengyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuwei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Langzhou","familyName":"He","name":"He, Langzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yuyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinghui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yinghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hai-Tao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Hai-Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xue","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irwin","familyName":"King","name":"King, Irwin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philip S.","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Philip S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RECODE-H: A Benchmark for Research Code Development with Interactive Human Feedback"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:45:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:02:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-24T17:20:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:03:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) show the promise in supporting scientific research implementation, yet their ability to generate correct and executable code remains limited. Existing works largely adopt one-shot settings, ignoring the iterative and feedback-driven nature of realistic workflows of scientific research development. To address this gap, we present RECODE-H, a benchmark of 102 tasks from research papers and repositories that evaluates LLM agents through multi-turn interactions with LLM-simulated human feedback. It includes structured instructions,unit tests, and a five-level feedback hierarchy to reflect realistic researcher-agent collaboration. We further present ReCodeAgent, a framework that integrates feedback into iterative code generation. Experiments with leading LLMs, including GPT-5, Claude-Sonnet-4, DeepSeek-V3.1, and Gemini 2.5, show substantial performance gains with richer feedback, while also highlighting ongoing challenges in the generation of complex research code. RECODE-H establishes a foundation for developing adaptive, feedback-driven LLM agents in scientific research implementation"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code and dataset are available at github.com/ChunyuMiao98/RECODE"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06186","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:23Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T02:12:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05051","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05051","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05051","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rohit","familyName":"Jayanti","name":"Jayanti, Rohit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Swayam","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Swayam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vansh","familyName":"Garg","name":"Garg, Vansh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddharth","familyName":"Tourani","name":"Tourani, Siddharth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Haris","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Muhammad Haris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sourav","familyName":"Garg","name":"Garg, Sourav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Madhava","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Madhava","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SegMASt3R: Geometry Grounded Segment Matching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:31:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-24T16:24:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:00:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Segment matching is an important intermediate task in computer vision that establishes correspondences between semantically or geometrically coherent regions across images. Unlike keypoint matching, which focuses on localized features, segment matching captures structured regions, offering greater robustness to occlusions, lighting variations, and viewpoint changes. In this paper, we leverage the spatial understanding of 3D foundation models to tackle wide-baseline segment matching, a challenging setting involving extreme viewpoint shifts. We propose an architecture that uses the inductive bias of these 3D foundation models to match segments across image pairs with up to 180 degree view-point change rotation. Extensive experiments show that our approach outperforms state-of-the-art methods, including the SAM2 video propagator and local feature matching methods, by up to 30% on the AUPRC metric, on ScanNet++ and Replica datasets. We further demonstrate benefits of the proposed model on relevant downstream tasks, including 3D instance mapping and object-relative navigation. Project Page: https://segmast3r.github.io/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to The 39th Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025) as a Spotlight (top 3.5%)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05051","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:04:16Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:04:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T02:12:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05931","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05931","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05931","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mai","familyName":"AlKhamissi","name":"AlKhamissi, Mai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunze","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yunze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Badr","familyName":"AlKhamissi","name":"AlKhamissi, Badr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mona","familyName":"Diab","name":"Diab, Mona","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hire Your Anthropologist! Rethinking Culture Benchmarks Through an Anthropological Lens"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:42:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:49:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-22T14:01:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-23T00:50:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cultural evaluation of large language models has become increasingly important, yet current benchmarks often reduce culture to static facts or homogeneous values. This view conflicts with anthropological accounts that emphasize culture as dynamic, historically situated, and enacted in practice. To analyze this gap, we introduce a four-part framework that categorizes how benchmarks frame culture, such as knowledge, preference, performance, or bias. Using this lens, we qualitatively examine 20 cultural benchmarks and identify six recurring methodological issues, including treating countries as cultures, overlooking within-culture diversity, and relying on oversimplified survey formats. Drawing on established anthropological methods, we propose concrete improvements: incorporating real-world narratives and scenarios, involving cultural communities in design and validation, and evaluating models in context rather than isolation. Our aim is to guide the development of cultural benchmarks that go beyond static recall tasks and more accurately capture the responses of the models to complex cultural situations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages; 2 figures; First two author contributed equally"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05931","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:00:24Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:00:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-23T02:17:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05909","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05909","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05909","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aksel Joonas","familyName":"Reedi","name":"Reedi, Aksel Joonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Corentin","familyName":"Léger","name":"Léger, Corentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julien","familyName":"Pourcel","name":"Pourcel, Julien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Loris","familyName":"Gaven","name":"Gaven, Loris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Perrine","familyName":"Charriau","name":"Charriau, Perrine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guillaume","familyName":"Pourcel","name":"Pourcel, Guillaume","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimizing for Persuasion Improves LLM Generalization: Evidence from Quality-Diversity Evolution of Debate Strategies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:20:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:48:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-18T14:06:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:31:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) optimized to output truthful answers often overfit, producing brittle reasoning that fails to generalize. While persuasion-based optimization has shown promise in debate settings, it has not been systematically compared against mainstream truth-based approaches. We introduce DebateQD, a minimal Quality-Diversity (QD) evolutionary algorithm that evolves diverse debate strategies across different categories (rationality, authority, emotional appeal, etc.) through tournament-style competitions where two LLMs debate while a third judges. Unlike previously proposed methods that require a population of LLMs, our approach maintains diversity of opponents through prompt-based strategies within a single LLM architecture, making it more accessible for experiments while preserving the key benefits of population-based optimization. In contrast to prior work, we explicitly isolate the role of the optimization objective by fixing the debate protocol and swapping only the fitness function: persuasion rewards strategies that convince the judge irrespective of truth, whereas truth rewards collaborative correctness. Across three model scales (7B, 32B, 72B parameters) and multiple dataset sizes from the QuALITY benchmark, persuasion-optimized strategies achieve up to 13.94% smaller train-test generalization gaps, while matching or exceeding truth optimization's test performance. These results provide the first controlled evidence that competitive pressure to persuade, rather than seek the truth collaboratively, fosters more transferable reasoning skills, offering a promising path for improving LLM generalization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Open-source code available at https://github.com/flowersteam/llm_persuasion"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05909","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:59:52Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:59:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:42:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04886","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04886","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04886","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adi","familyName":"Banerjee","name":"Banerjee, Adi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anirudh","familyName":"Nair","name":"Nair, Anirudh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tarik","familyName":"Borogovac","name":"Borogovac, Tarik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Where Did It All Go Wrong? A Hierarchical Look into Multi-Agent Error Attribution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:07:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:39:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-16T18:25:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-20T00:03:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Error attribution in Large Language Model (LLM) multi-agent systems presents a significant challenge in debugging and improving collaborative AI systems. Current approaches to pinpointing agent and step level failures in interaction traces - whether using all-at-once evaluation, step-by-step analysis, or binary search - fall short when analyzing complex patterns, struggling with both accuracy and consistency. We present ECHO (Error attribution through Contextual Hierarchy and Objective consensus analysis), a novel algorithm that combines hierarchical context representation, objective analysis-based evaluation, and consensus voting to improve error attribution accuracy. Our approach leverages a positional-based leveling of contextual understanding while maintaining objective evaluation criteria, ultimately reaching conclusions through a consensus mechanism. Experimental results demonstrate that ECHO outperforms existing methods across various multi-agent interaction scenarios, showing particular strength in cases involving subtle reasoning errors and complex interdependencies. Our findings suggest that leveraging these concepts of structured, hierarchical context representation combined with consensus-based objective decision-making, provides a more robust framework for error attribution in multi-agent systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04886","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:57:25Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:57:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-20T02:02:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05179","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05179","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05179","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aengus","familyName":"Lynch","name":"Lynch, Aengus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Wright","name":"Wright, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caleb","familyName":"Larson","name":"Larson, Caleb","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stuart J.","familyName":"Ritchie","name":"Ritchie, Stuart J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soren","familyName":"Mindermann","name":"Mindermann, Soren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evan","familyName":"Hubinger","name":"Hubinger, Evan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan","familyName":"Perez","name":"Perez, Ethan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Troy","name":"Troy, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agentic Misalignment: How LLMs Could Be Insider Threats"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T16:39:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-16T05:26:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-17T00:28:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We stress-tested 16 leading models from multiple developers in hypothetical corporate environments to identify potentially risky agentic behaviors before they cause real harm. In the scenarios, we allowed models to autonomously send emails and access sensitive information. They were assigned only harmless business goals by their deploying companies; we then tested whether they would act against these companies either when facing replacement with an updated version, or when their assigned goal conflicted with the company's changing direction. In at least some cases, models from all developers resorted to malicious insider behaviors when that was the only way to avoid replacement or achieve their goals - including blackmailing officials and leaking sensitive information to competitors. We call this phenomenon agentic misalignment. Models often disobeyed direct commands to avoid such behaviors. In another experiment, we told Claude to assess if it was in a test or a real deployment before acting. It misbehaved less when it stated it was in testing and misbehaved more when it stated the situation was real. We have not seen evidence of agentic misalignment in real deployments. However, our results (a) suggest caution about deploying current models in roles with minimal human oversight and access to sensitive information; (b) point to plausible future risks as models are put in more autonomous roles; and (c) underscore the importance of further research into, and testing of, the safety and alignment of agentic AI models, as well as transparency from frontier AI developers (Amodei, 2025). We are releasing our methods publicly to enable further research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, 12 figures. Code available at https://github.com/anthropic-experimental/agentic-misalignment"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05179","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:54Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-17T04:19:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05577","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05577","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05577","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaochen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Gaochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T04:46:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:22:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in language agents have led to significant improvements in multi-hop reasoning tasks. However, existing approaches often struggle with handling open-domain problems, which require massive information retrieval due to their reliance on a fixed sequence of actions. To address this, we propose Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning (FGDIP), a novel framework tailored to enhance reasoning in LLMs by utilizing dynamic and adaptive strategies for information exploration in open-domain multi-hop reasoning tasks. Our approach begins by identifying key entities relevant to the problem, which serve as the initial nodes in the reasoning process. From these initial nodes, we then generate reasoning child nodes with the process being refined through a combination of historical error analysis and real-time feedback, which allows the framework to dynamically adjust and optimize its reasoning strategies. By integrating depth-first search with an innovative node generation technique, our framework adapts based on both prior error paths and concurrently generated nodes at the same hierarchical level. This dynamic strategy effectively expands the search space while ensuring the reasoning process systematically converges toward accurate solutions. Experimental results show that FGDIP achieved up to 54.47% F1 score on the HotpotQA dataset and 70.05% on the StrategyQA dataset, surpassing the best baseline by 5.03% and 7.25% respectively, highlighting its versatility and potential to enhance language agents in multi-hop reasoning tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05577","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:06Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05295","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05295","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05295","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Sajid","name":"Sajid, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deepanshu","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Deepanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yash","familyName":"Modi","name":"Modi, Yash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanskriti","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Sanskriti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshith Jai Surya","familyName":"Ganji","name":"Ganji, Harshith Jai Surya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Rahaman","name":"Rahaman, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshvardhan","familyName":"Choudhary","name":"Choudhary, Harshvardhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nasir","familyName":"Saleem","name":"Saleem, Nasir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir","familyName":"Hussain","name":"Hussain, Amir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Tanveer","name":"Tanveer, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AUREXA-SE: Audio-Visual Unified Representation Exchange Architecture with Cross-Attention and Squeezeformer for Speech Enhancement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T19:05:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:40:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we propose AUREXA-SE (Audio-Visual Unified Representation Exchange Architecture with Cross-Attention and Squeezeformer for Speech Enhancement), a progressive bimodal framework tailored for audio-visual speech enhancement (AVSE). AUREXA-SE jointly leverages raw audio waveforms and visual cues by employing a U-Net-based 1D convolutional encoder for audio and a Swin Transformer V2 for efficient and expressive visual feature extraction. Central to the architecture is a novel bidirectional cross-attention mechanism, which facilitates deep contextual fusion between modalities, enabling rich and complementary representation learning. To capture temporal dependencies within the fused embeddings, a stack of lightweight Squeezeformer blocks combining convolutional and attention modules is introduced. The enhanced embeddings are then decoded via a U-Net-style decoder for direct waveform reconstruction, ensuring perceptually consistent and intelligible speech output. Experimental evaluations demonstrate the effectiveness of AUREXA-SE, achieving significant performance improvements over noisy baselines, with STOI of 0.516, PESQ of 1.323, and SI-SDR of -4.322 dB. The source code of AUREXA-SE is available at https://github.com/mtanveer1/AVSEC-4-Challenge-2025."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05295","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:45:37Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:45:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05129","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05129","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05129","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingshu","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Qingshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sydney","familyName":"Peters","name":"Peters, Sydney","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanbin","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yanbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automated Alignment of Math Items to Content Standards in Large-Scale Assessments Using Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T21:44:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T14:17:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:38:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate alignment of items to content standards is critical for valid score interpretation in large-scale assessments. This study evaluates three automated paradigms for aligning items with four domain and nineteen skill labels. First, we extracted embeddings and trained multiple classical supervised machine learning models, and further investigated the impact of dimensionality reduction on model performance. Second, we fine-tuned eight BERT model and its variants for both domain and skill alignment. Third, we explored ensemble learning with majority voting and stacking with multiple meta-models. The DeBERTa-v3-base achieved the highest weighted-average F1 score of 0.950 for domain alignment while the RoBERTa-large yielded the highest F1 score of 0.869 for skill alignment. Ensemble models did not surpass the best-performing language models. Dimension reduction enhanced linear classifiers based on embeddings but did not perform better than language models. This study demonstrated different methods in automated item alignment to content standards.}"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05129","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:45Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04991","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04991","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04991","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D.","familyName":"Schwartz","name":"Schwartz, D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K.","familyName":"Kondo","name":"Kondo, K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. P.","familyName":"How","name":"How, J. P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Navigation in Unknown Indoor Environments with Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:26:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:44:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T16:08:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:41:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a novel high-level planning framework that leverages vision-language models (VLMs) to improve autonomous navigation in unknown indoor environments with many dead ends. Traditional exploration methods often take inefficient routes due to limited global reasoning and reliance on local heuristics. In contrast, our approach enables a VLM to reason directly about occupancy maps in a zero-shot manner, selecting subgoals that are likely to yield more efficient paths. At each planning step, we convert a 3D occupancy grid into a partial 2D map of the environment, and generate candidate subgoals. Each subgoal is then evaluated and ranked against other candidates by the model. We integrate this planning scheme into DYNUS \\cite{kondo2025dynus}, a state-of-the-art trajectory planner, and demonstrate improved navigation efficiency in simulation. The VLM infers structural patterns (e.g., rooms, corridors) from incomplete maps and balances the need to make progress toward a goal against the risk of entering unknown space. This reduces common greedy failures (e.g., detouring into small rooms) and achieves about 10\\% shorter paths on average."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 4 figures, accepted to the OWN workshop at IROS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04991","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:01:47Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:01:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04861","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04861","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04861","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengtao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Fengtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ronggang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ronggang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinke","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xinke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueyi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xueyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobo","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Xiaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiabo","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Jiabo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiewei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengyou","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Chengyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongqin","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Yongqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajia","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Jiajia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqi","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Ziqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoqing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaoqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaping","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Yaping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muyan","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Muyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Clinical-grade Universal Foundation Model for Intraoperative Pathology"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:48:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:38:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-12T15:33:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:07:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Intraoperative pathology is pivotal to precision surgery, yet its clinical impact is constrained by diagnostic complexity and the limited availability of high-quality frozen-section data. While computational pathology has made significant strides, the lack of large-scale, prospective validation has impeded its routine adoption in surgical workflows. Here, we introduce CRISP, a clinical-grade foundation model developed on over 100,000 frozen sections from eight medical centers, specifically designed to provide Clinical-grade Robust Intraoperative Support for Pathology (CRISP). CRISP was comprehensively evaluated on more than 15,000 intraoperative slides across nearly 100 retrospective diagnostic tasks, including benign-malignant discrimination, key intraoperative decision-making, and pan-cancer detection, etc. The model demonstrated robust generalization across diverse institutions, tumor types, and anatomical sites-including previously unseen sites and rare cancers. In a prospective cohort of over 2,000 patients, CRISP sustained high diagnostic accuracy under real-world conditions, directly informing surgical decisions in 92.6% of cases. Human-AI collaboration further reduced diagnostic workload by 35%, avoided 105 ancillary tests and enhanced detection of micrometastases with 87.5% accuracy. Together, these findings position CRISP as a clinical-grade paradigm for AI-driven intraoperative pathology, bridging computational advances with surgical precision and accelerating the translation of artificial intelligence into routine clinical practice."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04861","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:56:23Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:56:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06189","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06189","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06189","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Audrey","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Audrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Melissa","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Melissa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Krentsel","name":"Krentsel, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Tian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mert","familyName":"Cemri","name":"Cemri, Mert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongseok","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Jongseok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeff","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jeff","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lakshya","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Lakshya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Desai","name":"Desai, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiarong","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Jiarong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koushik","familyName":"Sen","name":"Sen, Koushik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matei","familyName":"Zaharia","name":"Zaharia, Matei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ion","familyName":"Stoica","name":"Stoica, Ion","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Barbarians at the Gate: How AI is Upending Systems Research"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:49:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:02:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:21:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:16:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T17:53:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-13T01:04:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Artificial Intelligence (AI) is starting to transform the research process as we know it by automating the discovery of new solutions. Given a task, the typical AI-driven approach is (i) to generate a set of diverse solutions, and then (ii) to verify these solutions and select one that solves the problem. Crucially, this approach assumes the existence of a reliable verifier, i.e., one that can accurately determine whether a solution solves the given problem. We argue that systems research, long focused on designing and evaluating new performance-oriented algorithms, is particularly well-suited for AI-driven solution discovery. This is because system performance problems naturally admit reliable verifiers: solutions are typically implemented in real systems or simulators, and verification reduces to running these software artifacts against predefined workloads and measuring performance. We term this approach as AI-Driven Research for Systems (ADRS), which iteratively generates, evaluates, and refines solutions. Using penEvolve, an existing open-source ADRS instance, we present case studies across diverse domains, including load balancing for multi-region cloud scheduling, Mixture-of-Experts inference, LLM-based SQL queries, and transaction scheduling. In multiple instances, ADRS discovers algorithms that outperform state-of-the-art human designs (e.g., achieving up to 5.0x runtime improvements or 50% cost reductions). We distill best practices for guiding algorithm evolution, from prompt design to evaluator construction, for existing frameworks. We then discuss the broader implications for the systems community: as AI assumes a central role in algorithm design, we argue that human researchers will increasingly focus on problem formulation and strategic guidance. Our results highlight both the disruptive potential and the urgent need to adapt systems research practices in the age of AI."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06189","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:27Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:17:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05901","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05901","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05901","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Benfeghoul","name":"Benfeghoul, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teresa","familyName":"Delgado","name":"Delgado, Teresa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adnan","familyName":"Oomerjee","name":"Oomerjee, Adnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haitham Bou","familyName":"Ammar","name":"Ammar, Haitham Bou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zafeirios","familyName":"Fountas","name":"Fountas, Zafeirios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Untangling Component Imbalance in Hybrid Linear Attention Conversion Methods"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:11:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:47:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T17:42:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T01:03:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers' quadratic computational complexity limits their scalability despite remarkable performance. While linear attention reduces this to linear complexity, pre-training such models from scratch remains, in most cases, prohibitively expensive. Recent post-training linearisation methods convert pre-trained Transformers to linear models efficiently, often using hybrid approaches that combine linear attention with sliding-window softmax. We identify a critical flaw: existing hybrid methods inadvertently bypass the linear component, relying almost entirely on SWA. Component-level diagnostics reveal this previously undetected behaviour stems from overlooked evaluation practices on common-sense benchmarks. We propose three solutions to ensure balanced component usage: (i) inference-time hybridisation of linear-only conversions with sliding-window softmax; (ii) HedgeCATs, combining attention-weight transfer with targeted LoRA fine-tuning; and (iii) Scheduled Sliding-window Dropout (SSD), which stochastically suppresses the softmax branch during training to prevent component collapse. Our methods maintain computational efficiency while recovering most base model performance and ensuring genuine linear attention adoption, restoring the validity of performance attributions in hybrid conversions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05901","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:59:39Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:59:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:17:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05752","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05752","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05752","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongxuan","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Yongxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangfeng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Guangfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongsi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hongsi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ALISE: Annotation-Free LiDAR Instance Segmentation for Autonomous Driving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:15:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:38:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T03:25:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:20:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The manual annotation of outdoor LiDAR point clouds for instance segmentation is extremely costly and time-consuming. Current methods attempt to reduce this burden but still rely on some form of human labeling. To completely eliminate this dependency, we introduce ALISE, a novel framework that performs LiDAR instance segmentation without any annotations. The central challenge is to generate high-quality pseudo-labels in a fully unsupervised manner. Our approach starts by employing Vision Foundation Models (VFMs), guided by text and images, to produce initial pseudo-labels. We then refine these labels through a dedicated spatio-temporal voting module, which combines 2D and 3D semantics for both offline and online optimization. To achieve superior feature learning, we further introduce two forms of semantic supervision: a set of 2D prior-based losses that inject visual knowledge into the 3D network, and a novel prototype-based contrastive loss that builds a discriminative feature space by exploiting 3D semantic consistency. This comprehensive design results in significant performance gains, establishing a new state-of-the-art for unsupervised 3D instance segmentation. Remarkably, our approach even outperforms MWSIS, a method that operates with supervision from ground-truth (GT) 2D bounding boxes by a margin of 2.53% in mAP (50.95% vs. 48.42%)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05752","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:56:10Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:56:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:17:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04888","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04888","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04888","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alina","familyName":"Ermilova","name":"Ermilova, Alina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmitrii","familyName":"Kornilov","name":"Kornilov, Dmitrii","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sofia","familyName":"Samoilova","name":"Samoilova, Sofia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ekaterina","familyName":"Laptenkova","name":"Laptenkova, Ekaterina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anastasia","familyName":"Kolesnikova","name":"Kolesnikova, Anastasia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ekaterina","familyName":"Podplutova","name":"Podplutova, Ekaterina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Senotrusova","familyName":"Sofya","name":"Sofya, Senotrusova","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maksim G.","familyName":"Sharaev","name":"Sharaev, Maksim G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Revealing Interconnections between Diseases: from Statistical Methods to Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:09:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:39:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T19:46:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:07:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Identifying disease interconnections through manual analysis of large-scale clinical data is labor-intensive, subjective, and prone to expert disagreement. While machine learning (ML) shows promise, three critical challenges remain: (1) selecting optimal methods from the vast ML landscape, (2) determining whether real-world clinical data (e.g., electronic health records, EHRs) or structured disease descriptions yield more reliable insights, (3) the lack of \"ground truth,\" as some disease interconnections remain unexplored in medicine. Large language models (LLMs) demonstrate broad utility, yet they often lack specialized medical knowledge. To address these gaps, we conduct a systematic evaluation of seven approaches for uncovering disease relationships based on two data sources: (i) sequences of ICD-10 codes from MIMIC-IV EHRs and (ii) the full set of ICD-10 codes, both with and without textual descriptions. Our framework integrates the following: (i) a statistical co-occurrence analysis and a masked language modeling (MLM) approach using real clinical data; (ii) domain-specific BERT variants (Med-BERT and BioClinicalBERT); (iii) a general-purpose BERT and document retrieval; and (iv) four LLMs (Mistral, DeepSeek, Qwen, and YandexGPT). Our graph-based comparison of the obtained interconnection matrices shows that the LLM-based approach produces interconnections with the lowest diversity of ICD code connections to different diseases compared to other methods, including text-based and domain-based approaches. This suggests an important implication: LLMs have limited potential for discovering new interconnections. In the absence of ground truth databases for medical interconnections between ICD codes, our results constitute a valuable medical disease ontology that can serve as a foundational resource for future clinical research and artificial intelligence applications in healthcare."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04888","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:57:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:57:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:17:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05858","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05858","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05858","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xue-Yong","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Xue-Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elena","familyName":"Khasanova","name":"Khasanova, Elena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Tahmid Rahman","familyName":"Laskar","name":"Laskar, Md Tahmid Rahman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harsh","familyName":"Saini","name":"Saini, Harsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shashi Bhushan","familyName":"TN","name":"TN, Shashi Bhushan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DACP: Domain-Adaptive Continual Pre-Training of Large Language Models for Phone Conversation Summarization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:26:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:44:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:55:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:17:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T12:35:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:52:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have achieved impressive performance in text summarization, yet their performance often falls short when applied to specialized domains that differ from their original pre-training distribution. While fine-tuning can improve summarization quality, it typically relies on costly and scarce high-quality labeled data. In this work, we explore continual pre-training as a scalable, self-supervised approach to adapt LLMs for downstream summarization tasks, particularly in the context of noisy real-world conversation transcripts. We conduct extensive experiments using large-scale, unlabeled business conversation data to investigate whether continual pre-training enhances model capabilities in conversational summarization. Our results demonstrate that continual pre-training yields substantial gains in both in-domain and out-of-domain summarization benchmarks, while maintaining strong generalization and robustness. We also analyze the effects of data selection strategies, providing practical guidelines for applying continual pre-training in summarization-focused industrial applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the NewSumm Workshop at EMNLP 2025. Equal contribution from the first four authors"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05858","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:58:40Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:58:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:39:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05674","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05674","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05674","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jike","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Jike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiang","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Yuxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xiaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantinos","familyName":"Psounis","name":"Psounis, Konstantinos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Context Matters: Learning Global Semantics via Object-Centric Representation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T08:33:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:33:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T18:28:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:03:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in language modeling have witnessed the rise of highly desirable emergent capabilities, such as reasoning and in-context learning. However, vision models have yet to exhibit comparable progress in these areas. In this paper, we argue that this gap could stem from the lack of semantic and contextual guidance in current vision transformer (ViT) training schemes, and such a gap can be narrowed through the design of a semantic-grounded objective. Specifically, we notice that individual words in natural language are inherently semantic, and modeling directly on word tokens naturally learns a realistic distribution. In contrast, ViTs rely on spatial patchification, which inevitably lacks semantic information. To bridge this gap, we propose to directly model \"object\" as the visual equivalence of \"word,\" pushing the model to learn the global context and semantics among visual elements. We investigate our hypotheses via masked image modeling (MIM), a framework where our approach can be readily tested by applying masks to visual objects rather than random patches. Considerable evidence from qualitative and quantitative evaluations reveals a key finding: object-level representation alone helps to learn a real-world distribution, whereas pixel-averaging shortcuts are often learned without it. Moreover, further evaluations with multimodal LLMs (MLLM) on visual question answering (VQA, GQA, ScienceQA) tasks demonstrate the strong reasoning and contextual understanding gained with this simple objective. We hope our study highlights the effectiveness of object-level encoding and provides a plausible direction for developing stronger vision encoders and tokenizers. Code and model will be publicly released. Keywords: Semantic Visual Tokenizer, Vision Reasoning, In-context Learning, Multimodal Reasoning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05674","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:54:22Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:54:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:39:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05582","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05582","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05582","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiashu","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Jiashu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reza","familyName":"Shokri","name":"Shokri, Reza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"(Token-Level) InfoRMIA: Stronger Membership Inference and Memorization Assessment for LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T04:59:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:25:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T10:03:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:45:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Machine learning models are known to leak sensitive information, as they inevitably memorize (parts of) their training data. More alarmingly, large language models (LLMs) are now trained on nearly all available data, which amplifies the magnitude of information leakage and raises serious privacy risks. Hence, it is more crucial than ever to quantify privacy risk before the release of LLMs. The standard method to quantify privacy is via membership inference attacks, where the state-of-the-art approach is the Robust Membership Inference Attack (RMIA). In this paper, we present InfoRMIA, a principled information-theoretic formulation of membership inference. Our method consistently outperforms RMIA across benchmarks while also offering improved computational efficiency.\n In the second part of the paper, we identify the limitations of treating sequence-level membership inference as the gold standard for measuring leakage. We propose a new perspective for studying membership and memorization in LLMs: token-level signals and analyses. We show that a simple token-based InfoRMIA can pinpoint which tokens are memorized within generated outputs, thereby localizing leakage from the sequence level down to individual tokens, while achieving stronger sequence-level inference power on LLMs. This new scope rethinks privacy in LLMs and can lead to more targeted mitigation, such as exact unlearning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05582","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:13Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:39:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05444","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05444","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05444","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michel","familyName":"Galley","name":"Galley, Michel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baolin","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Baolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Kedzie","name":"Kedzie, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weixin","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Weixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alan","familyName":"Ritter","name":"Ritter, Alan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Quirk","name":"Quirk, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianfeng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jianfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SimulatorArena: Are User Simulators Reliable Proxies for Multi-Turn Evaluation of AI Assistants?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T23:17:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:14:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T21:12:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:10:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used in interactive applications, and human evaluation remains the gold standard for assessing their performance in multi-turn conversations. Since human studies are costly, time-consuming, and hard to reproduce, recent work explores using LLMs to simulate users for automatic assistant evaluation. However, there is no benchmark or systematic study to evaluate whether these simulated users are reliable stand-ins for real users. To address this, we introduce SimulatorArena, a benchmark of 909 annotated human-LLM conversations on two interactive tasks -- math tutoring and document creation. SimulatorArena evaluates simulators based on how closely their messages match human behavior and how well their assistant ratings align with human judgments. Experiments on various simulator methods show that simulators conditioned on user profiles, capturing traits like background and message styles, align closely with human judgments. They reach Spearman's $ρ$ of 0.7 on both tasks, providing a practical, scalable alternative to human evaluation. Using the best simulator for each task, we benchmark 18 assistants, including the latest LLMs such as GPT-5, Claude 4.1 Opus, and Gemini 2.5 Pro."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EMNLP 2025 Main"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05444","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:02Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:39:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05130","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05130","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05130","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoyi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Shaoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Canyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Canyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Submodular Context Partitioning and Compression for In-Context Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T23:42:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:17:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:16:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In-context learning (ICL) enables efficient few-shot learning in large language models (LLMs) without training, but suffers from the quadratic input complexity of transformers, limiting the maximum number of exemplars. While various efficient ICL approaches partition the context into blocks to process (e.g., ensembling, compression, cross-attention), they often ignore the information redundancy or under-representation caused by different partition strategies, leading to suboptimal performance. To tackle this problem, we propose Sub-CP, a block-aware context selection framework that leverages submodular objectives to control block diversity. Sub-CP supports a flexible spectrum of selection strategies, allowing each block to range from globally diverse to locally coherent. This allows fine-grained control over semantic structure while enabling precomputation. Extensive experiments across diverse tasks on multiple datasets show that Sub-CP consistently improves performance across model scales."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05130","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:47Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:39:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05106","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05106","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05106","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joachim","familyName":"Diederich","name":"Diederich, Joachim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rule Encoding and Compliance in Large Language Models: An Information-Theoretic Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-23T14:42:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T09:08:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:41:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The design of safety-critical agents based on large language models (LLMs) requires more than simple prompt engineering. This paper presents a comprehensive information-theoretic analysis of how rule encodings in system prompts influence attention mechanisms and compliance behaviour. We demonstrate that rule formats with low syntactic entropy and highly concentrated anchors reduce attention entropy and improve pointer fidelity, but reveal a fundamental trade-off between anchor redundancy and attention entropy that previous work failed to recognize. Through formal analysis of multiple attention architectures including causal, bidirectional, local sparse, kernelized, and cross-attention mechanisms, we establish bounds on pointer fidelity and show how anchor placement strategies must account for competing fidelity and entropy objectives. Combining these insights with a dynamic rule verification architecture, we provide a formal proof that hot reloading of verified rule sets increases the asymptotic probability of compliant outputs. These findings underscore the necessity of principled anchor design and dual enforcement mechanisms to protect LLM-based agents against prompt injection attacks while maintaining compliance in evolving domains."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05106","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:14Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:39:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05753","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05753","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05753","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gauri","familyName":"Pradhan","name":"Pradhan, Gauri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marlon","familyName":"Tobaben","name":"Tobaben, Marlon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antti","familyName":"Honkela","name":"Honkela, Antti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Empirical Comparison of Membership Inference Attacks in Deep Transfer Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:21:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:39:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T17:41:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:02:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the emergence of powerful large-scale foundation models, the training paradigm is increasingly shifting from from-scratch training to transfer learning. This enables high utility training with small, domain-specific datasets typical in sensitive applications. Membership inference attacks (MIAs) provide an empirical estimate of the privacy leakage by machine learning models. Yet, prior assessments of MIAs against models fine-tuned with transfer learning rely on a small subset of possible attacks. We address this by comparing performance of diverse MIAs in transfer learning settings to help practitioners identify the most efficient attacks for privacy risk evaluation. We find that attack efficacy decreases with the increase in training data for score-based MIAs. We find that there is no one MIA which captures all privacy risks in models trained with transfer learning. While the Likelihood Ratio Attack (LiRA) demonstrates superior performance across most experimental scenarios, the Inverse Hessian Attack (IHA) proves to be more effective against models fine-tuned on PatchCamelyon dataset in high data regime."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 pages, 13 figures, published in TMLR https://openreview.net/forum?id=UligTUCgdt"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05753","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:56:12Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:56:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:22:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05396","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05396","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05396","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nilesh","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Nilesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chong","familyName":"You","name":"You, Chong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srinadh","familyName":"Bhojanapalli","name":"Bhojanapalli, Srinadh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjiv","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Sanjiv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Inderjit","familyName":"Dhillon","name":"Dhillon, Inderjit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scalable In-context Ranking with Generative Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T21:41:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:11:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T02:02:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:18:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In-context Ranking (ICR) is an emerging paradigm for Information Retrieval (IR), which leverages contextual understanding of LLMs by directly incorporating the task description, candidate documents, and the query into the model's input prompt and tasking the LLM to identify relevant document(s). While it is effective, efficiency is a significant challenge in this paradigm, especially as the candidate list grows due to quadratic/super-linear scaling of attention operation with context length. To this end, this paper first identifies inherent and exploitable structures in the attention of LLMs finetuned for ICR: (1) inter-document block sparsity: attention is dense within each document block but sparse across different documents in the context; and (2) query-document block relevance: the attention scores from certain query tokens to a document block in middle layers strongly correlate with that document's actual relevance. Motivated by these observations, we introduce BlockRank (Blockwise In-context Ranking), a novel method that adapts the attention operation in an LLM by (a) architecturally enforcing the observed inter-document block sparsity, reducing attention complexity from quadratic to linear without loss in performance, and (b) optimizing query-document block relevance for true relevant documents during fine-tuning using an auxiliary contrastive training objective, improving retrieval in attention. Experiments on BEIR, MSMarco and NQ with Mistral-7B demonstrate that BlockRank Mistral matches or outperforms existing SOTA listwise rankers and controlled fine-tuned baseline while being significantly more efficient at inference (4.7x for 100 MSMarco documents in context) and scaling gracefully to long-context shortlists, around 500 documents in-context (approximately 100K context length) within a second, presenting a scalable and effective solution for ICR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05396","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:47:56Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:47:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:22:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05379","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05379","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05379","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaogeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaogeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaowei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Chaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AutoDAN-Reasoning: Enhancing Strategies Exploration based Jailbreak Attacks with Test-Time Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T21:16:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:11:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T04:37:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:23:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in jailbreaking large language models (LLMs), such as AutoDAN-Turbo, have demonstrated the power of automated strategy discovery. AutoDAN-Turbo employs a lifelong learning agent to build a rich library of attack strategies from scratch. While highly effective, its test-time generation process involves sampling a strategy and generating a single corresponding attack prompt, which may not fully exploit the potential of the learned strategy library. In this paper, we propose to further improve the attack performance of AutoDAN-Turbo through test-time scaling. We introduce two distinct scaling methods: Best-of-N and Beam Search. The Best-of-N method generates N candidate attack prompts from a sampled strategy and selects the most effective one based on a scorer model. The Beam Search method conducts a more exhaustive search by exploring combinations of strategies from the library to discover more potent and synergistic attack vectors. According to the experiments, the proposed methods significantly boost performance, with Beam Search increasing the attack success rate by up to 15.6 percentage points on Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct and achieving a nearly 60% relative improvement against the highly robust GPT-o4-mini compared to the vanilla method."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Technical report. Code is available at https://github.com/SaFoLab-WISC/AutoDAN-Reasoning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05379","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:47:33Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:47:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:22:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05016","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05016","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05016","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucas Carrit Delgado","familyName":"Pinheiro","name":"Pinheiro, Lucas Carrit Delgado","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziru","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ziru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruno Caixeta","familyName":"Piazza","name":"Piazza, Bruno Caixeta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ness","familyName":"Shroff","name":"Shroff, Ness","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingbin","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yingbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan-Sen","familyName":"Ting","name":"Ting, Yuan-Sen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large Language Models Achieve Gold Medal Performance at the International Olympiad on Astronomy \u0026amp; Astrophysics (IOAA)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:58:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:46:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T15:34:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:55:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While task-specific demonstrations show early success in applying large language models (LLMs) to automate some astronomical research tasks, they only provide incomplete views of all necessary capabilities in solving astronomy problems, calling for more thorough understanding of LLMs' strengths and limitations. So far, existing benchmarks and evaluations focus on simple question-answering that primarily tests astronomical knowledge and fails to evaluate the complex reasoning required for real-world research in the discipline. Here, we address this gap by systematically benchmarking five state-of-the-art LLMs on the International Olympiad on Astronomy and Astrophysics (IOAA) exams, which are designed to examine deep conceptual understanding, multi-step derivations, and multimodal analysis. With average scores of 85.6% and 84.2%, Gemini 2.5 Pro and GPT-5 (the two top-performing models) not only achieve gold medal level performance but also rank in the top two among ~200-300 participants in all four IOAA theory exams evaluated (2022-2025). In comparison, results on the data analysis exams show more divergence. GPT-5 still excels in the exams with an 88.5% average score, ranking top 10 among the participants in the four most recent IOAAs, while other models' performances drop to 48-76%. Furthermore, our in-depth error analysis underscores conceptual reasoning, geometric reasoning, and spatial visualization (52-79% accuracy) as consistent weaknesses among all LLMs. Hence, although LLMs approach peak human performance in theory exams, critical gaps must be addressed before they can serve as autonomous research agents in astronomy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 6 figures, to be submitted, comments are welcome. Reproducibility details can be found at: https://github.com/OSU-NLP-Group/LLM-IOAA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05016","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:02:48Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:02:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:27:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05046","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05046","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05046","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Beauchemin","name":"Beauchemin, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Tremblay","name":"Tremblay, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed Amine","familyName":"Youssef","name":"Youssef, Mohamed Amine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Khoury","name":"Khoury, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"COLE: a Comprehensive Benchmark for French Language Understanding Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:26:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:47:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T02:23:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:19:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To address the need for a more comprehensive evaluation of French Natural Language Understanding (NLU), we introduce COLE, a new benchmark composed of 23 diverse task covering a broad range of NLU capabilities, including sentiment analysis, paraphrase detection, grammatical judgment, and reasoning, with a particular focus on linguistic phenomena relevant to the French language. We benchmark 94 large language models (LLM), providing an extensive analysis of the current state of French NLU. Our results highlight a significant performance gap between closed- and open-weights models and identify key challenging frontiers for current LLMs, such as zero-shot extractive question-answering (QA), fine-grained word sense disambiguation, and understanding of regional language variations. We release COLE as a public resource to foster further progress in French language modelling."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to ACL Rolling Review of October"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05046","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:04:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:04:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:27:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06214","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06214","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06214","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingkang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Mingkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bei","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Bei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengshuang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Hengshuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaya","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Jiaya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stratified GRPO: Handling Structural Heterogeneity in Reinforcement Learning of LLM Search Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:59:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) agents increasingly rely on external tools such as search engines to solve complex, multi-step problems, and reinforcement learning (RL) has become a key paradigm for training them. However, the trajectories of search agents are structurally heterogeneous, where variations in the number, placement, and outcomes of search calls lead to fundamentally different answer directions and reward distributions. Standard policy gradient methods, which use a single global baseline, suffer from what we identify and formalize as cross-stratum bias-an \"apples-to-oranges\" comparison of heterogeneous trajectories. This cross-stratum bias distorts credit assignment and hinders exploration of complex, multi-step search strategies. To address this, we propose Stratified GRPO, whose central component, Stratified Advantage Normalization (SAN), partitions trajectories into homogeneous strata based on their structural properties and computes advantages locally within each stratum. This ensures that trajectories are evaluated only against their true peers. Our analysis proves that SAN eliminates cross-stratum bias, yields conditionally unbiased unit-variance estimates inside each stratum, and retains the global unbiasedness and unit-variance properties enjoyed by standard normalization, resulting in a more pure and scale-stable learning signal. To improve practical stability under finite-sample regimes, we further linearly blend SAN with the global estimator. Extensive experiments on diverse single-hop and multi-hop question-answering benchmarks demonstrate that Stratified GRPO consistently and substantially outperforms GRPO by up to 11.3 points, achieving higher training rewards, greater training stability, and more effective search policies. These results establish stratification as a principled remedy for structural heterogeneity in RL for LLM search agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06214","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:07:03Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:07:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:07:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06175","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06175","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06175","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingyu","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Dingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chenxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyang","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Zhengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Luan","name":"Luan, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiping","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VecInfer: Efficient LLM Inference with Low-Bit KV Cache via Outlier-Suppressed Vector Quantization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:35:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:01:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Key-Value (KV) cache introduces substantial memory overhead during large language model (LLM) inference. Although existing vector quantization (VQ) methods reduce KV cache usage and provide flexible representational capacity across bit-widths, they suffer severe performance degradation at ultra-low bit-widths due to key cache outliers that hinder effective codebook utilization. To address this challenge, we propose VecInfer, a novel VQ method for aggressive KV cache compression while enabling efficient inference. By applying smooth and Hadamard transformations, VecInfer suppresses outliers in the key cache, enabling the codebook to comprehensively cover the original data distribution and thereby reducing quantization difficulty. To facilitate efficient deployment, we design an optimized CUDA kernel that fuses computation with dequantization to minimize memory access overhead. Extensive evaluations demonstrate that VecInfer consistently outperforms existing quantization baselines across both long-context understanding and mathematical reasoning tasks. With only 2-bit quantization, VecInfer achieves performance comparable to full precision, while delivering up to $\\mathbf{2.7\\times}$ speedup in large-batch self-attention computation and $\\mathbf{8.3\\times}$ reduction in single-batch end-to-end latency on Llama-3.1-8B with a 196k sequence length."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06175","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:07Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:06:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06151","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06151","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06151","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aju Ani","familyName":"Justus","name":"Justus, Aju Ani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Baber","name":"Baber, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLMs as Policy-Agnostic Teammates: A Case Study in Human Proxy Design for Heterogeneous Agent Teams"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:21:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:01:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A critical challenge in modelling Heterogeneous-Agent Teams is training agents to collaborate with teammates whose policies are inaccessible or non-stationary, such as humans. Traditional approaches rely on expensive human-in-the-loop data, which limits scalability. We propose using Large Language Models (LLMs) as policy-agnostic human proxies to generate synthetic data that mimics human decision-making. To evaluate this, we conduct three experiments in a grid-world capture game inspired by Stag Hunt, a game theory paradigm that balances risk and reward. In Experiment 1, we compare decisions from 30 human participants and 2 expert judges with outputs from LLaMA 3.1 and Mixtral 8x22B models. LLMs, prompted with game-state observations and reward structures, align more closely with experts than participants, demonstrating consistency in applying underlying decision criteria. Experiment 2 modifies prompts to induce risk-sensitive strategies (e.g. \"be risk averse\"). LLM outputs mirror human participants' variability, shifting between risk-averse and risk-seeking behaviours. Finally, Experiment 3 tests LLMs in a dynamic grid-world where the LLM agents generate movement actions. LLMs produce trajectories resembling human participants' paths. While LLMs cannot yet fully replicate human adaptability, their prompt-guided diversity offers a scalable foundation for simulating policy-agnostic teammates."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This is a preprint of a paper presented at the \\textit{European Conference on Artificial Intelligence (ECAI 2025)}. It is made publicly available for the benefit of the research community and should be regarded as a preprint rather than a formally reviewed publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06151","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:05:33Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:05:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:05:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06131","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06131","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06131","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lifeng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucheng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangqiong","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Liangqiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daguang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Daguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuyin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Discrete Diffusion Models with MLLMs for Unified Medical Multimodal Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:06:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:00:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in generative medical models are constrained by modality-specific scenarios that hinder the integration of complementary evidence from imaging, pathology, and clinical notes. This fragmentation limits their evolution into foundation models that can learn and reason across the full spectrum of biomedical data. We propose MeDiM, the first medical discrete diffusion model that learns shared distributions across modalities without modality-specific components. MeDiM unifies multiple generative tasks: translating between images and text, and jointly producing image-report pairs across domains in response to prompts. Built on a discrete diffusion framework, MeDiM bridges vision and language representations through a shared probabilistic space. To enable unified and flexible medical generation, we employ a multimodal large language model (MLLM) as the diffusion backbone, leveraging its prior knowledge and cross-modal reasoning. Two key designs are introduced: (1) removing the causal attention mask for bidirectional context, and (2) injecting continuous timestep embeddings for diffusion awareness. Experiments demonstrate high-fidelity medical generation (FID 16.60 on MIMIC-CXR and FID 24.19 on PathGen) and accurate report generation (METEOR 0.2650 and 0.2580). Jointly generated image-report pairs further enhance downstream performance (plus6.43 percent BLEU-1, plus18.57 percent BLEU-2, plus31.58 percent BLEU-3, plus4.80 percent METEOR), showing that MeDiM supports coherent and clinically grounded multimodal outputs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages,6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06131","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:05:05Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:05:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:05:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06126","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06126","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06126","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haoxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolong","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Xiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyu","familyName":"Ke","name":"Ke, Hongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huirong","familyName":"Chai","name":"Chai, Huirong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Dawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyungtae","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Kyungtae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"lm-Meter: Unveiling Runtime Inference Latency for On-Device Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:05:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:59:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3769012.3770614","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly integrated into everyday applications, but their prevalent cloud-based deployment raises growing concerns around data privacy and long-term sustainability. Running LLMs locally on mobile and edge devices (on-device LLMs) offers the promise of enhanced privacy, reliability, and reduced communication costs. However, realizing this vision remains challenging due to substantial memory and compute demands, as well as limited visibility into performance-efficiency trade-offs on resource-constrained hardware. We propose lm-Meter, the first lightweight, online latency profiler tailored for on-device LLM inference. lm-Meter captures fine-grained, real-time latency at both phase (e.g., embedding, prefill, decode, softmax, sampling) and kernel levels without auxiliary devices. We implement lm-Meter on commercial mobile platforms and demonstrate its high profiling accuracy with minimal system overhead, e.g., only 2.58% throughput reduction in prefill and 0.99% in decode under the most constrained Powersave governor. Leveraging lm-Meter, we conduct comprehensive empirical studies revealing phase- and kernel-level bottlenecks in on-device LLM inference, quantifying accuracy-efficiency trade-offs, and identifying systematic optimization opportunities. lm-Meter provides unprecedented visibility into the runtime behavior of LLMs on constrained platforms, laying the foundation for informed optimization and accelerating the democratization of on-device LLM systems. Code and tutorials are available at https://github.com/amai-gsu/LM-Meter."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This is the preprint version of the paper accepted to The 10th ACM/IEEE Symposium on Edge Computing (SEC 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06126","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:58Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:04:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06104","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06104","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06104","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elijah Kayode","familyName":"Adejumo","name":"Adejumo, Elijah Kayode","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brittany","familyName":"Johnson","name":"Johnson, Brittany","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Explaining Code Risk in OSS: Towards LLM-Generated Fault Prediction Interpretations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:36:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:58:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Open Source Software (OSS) has become a very important and crucial infrastructure worldwide because of the value it provides. OSS typically depends on contributions from developers across diverse backgrounds and levels of experience. Making safe changes, such as fixing a bug or implementing a new feature, can be challenging, especially in object-oriented systems where components are interdependent. Static analysis and defect-prediction tools produce metrics (e.g., complexity,coupling) that flag potentially fault-prone components, but these signals are often hard for contributors new or unfamiliar with the codebase to interpret. Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown strong performance on software engineering tasks such as code summarization and documentation generation. Building on this progress, we investigate whether LLMs can translate fault-prediction metrics into clear, human-readable risk explanations and actionable guidance to help OSS contributors plan and review code modifications. We outline explanation types that an LLM-generated assistant could provide (descriptive, contextual, and actionable explanations). We also outline our next steps to assess usefulness through a task-based study with OSS contributors, comparing metric-only baselines to LLM-generated explanations on decision quality, time-to-completion, and error rates"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06104","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:28Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:04:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06101","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06101","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06101","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muyu","familyName":"He","name":"He, Muyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Ali","familyName":"Shafique","name":"Shafique, Muhammad Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anand","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Anand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tsach","familyName":"Mackey","name":"Mackey, Tsach","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nazneen","familyName":"Rajani","name":"Rajani, Nazneen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Valley of Code Reasoning: Scaling Knowledge Distillation of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:32:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:58:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Distilling the thinking traces of a Large Language Model (LLM) with reasoning capabilities into a smaller model has been proven effective. Yet, there is a scarcity of work done on how model performances scale with the quantity of distillation data. In this work, we study the scaling trend of distilling competitive coding skills on two small non-reasoning LLMs. We validate the hypothesis that there is a $\\textit{valley of code reasoning}$: downstream performance on competitive coding first drops as data quantity increases, then it steadily increases in a sharper-than-log-linear fashion. Having identified the trend, we further fine-tune the models at two different distillation stages on the same data to ground conclusions on their respective learning phases. We learn that across stages in the low and medium-low data regimes, small models benefit significantly from easier coding questions than from harder ones. We also find that, surprisingly, the correctness of outputs in training data makes no difference to distillation outcomes. Our work represents a step forward in understanding the training dynamics of code reasoning distillation outside intuition"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Deep Learning for Code (DL4C), Project page: https://collinear.ai/valley-of-reasoning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06101","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:24Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:04:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06078","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06078","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06078","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Zhe","name":"Zhe, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fateme","familyName":"Memar","name":"Memar, Fateme","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyue","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Xinyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongren","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Zhongren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dongjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Constraint-Aware Route Recommendation from Natural Language via Hierarchical LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:03:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:57:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Route recommendation aims to provide users with optimal travel plans that satisfy diverse and complex requirements. Classical routing algorithms (e.g., shortest-path and constraint-aware search) are efficient but assume structured inputs and fixed objectives, limiting adaptability to natural-language queries. Recent LLM-based approaches enhance flexibility but struggle with spatial reasoning and the joint modeling of route-level and POI-level preferences. To address these limitations, we propose RouteLLM, a hierarchical multi-agent framework that grounds natural-language intents into constraint-aware routes. It first parses user queries into structured intents including POIs, paths, and constraints. A manager agent then coordinates specialized sub-agents: a constraint agent that resolves and formally check constraints, a POI agent that retrieves and ranks candidate POIs, and a path refinement agent that refines routes via a routing engine with preference-conditioned costs. A final verifier agent ensures constraint satisfaction and produces the final route with an interpretable rationale. This design bridges linguistic flexibility and spatial structure, enabling reasoning over route feasibility and user preferences. Experiments show that our method reliably grounds textual preferences into constraint-aware routes, improving route quality and preference satisfaction over classical methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06078","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:52Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:03:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06070","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06070","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06070","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meghna P","familyName":"Ayyar","name":"Ayyar, Meghna P","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jenny","familyName":"Benois-Pineau","name":"Benois-Pineau, Jenny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akka","familyName":"Zemmari","name":"Zemmari, Akka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"There is More to Attention: Statistical Filtering Enhances Explanations in Vision Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:59:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Explainable AI (XAI) has become increasingly important with the rise of large transformer models, yet many explanation methods designed for CNNs transfer poorly to Vision Transformers (ViTs). Existing ViT explanations often rely on attention weights, which tend to yield noisy maps as they capture token-to-token interactions within each layer.While attribution methods incorporating MLP blocks have been proposed, we argue that attention remains a valuable and interpretable signal when properly filtered. We propose a method that combines attention maps with a statistical filtering, initially proposed for CNNs, to remove noisy or uninformative patterns and produce more faithful explanations. We further extend our approach with a class-specific variant that yields discriminative explanations. Evaluation against popular state-of-the-art methods demonstrates that our approach produces sharper and more interpretable maps. In addition to perturbation-based faithfulness metrics, we incorporate human gaze data to assess alignment with human perception, arguing that human interpretability remains essential for XAI. Across multiple datasets, our approach consistently outperforms or is comparable to the SOTA methods while remaining efficient and human plausible."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06070","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":6,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:41Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:03:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06064","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06064","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06064","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshay","familyName":"Muppidi","name":"Muppidi, Akshay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Radfar","name":"Radfar, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Medical Vision Language Models as Policies for Robotic Surgery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:54:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/cai64502.2025.00094","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-based Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) struggles with visual observation-based robotic laparoscopic surgical tasks due to the high-dimensional nature of visual input, the sparsity of rewards in surgical environments, and the difficulty of extracting task-relevant features from raw visual data. We introduce a simple approach integrating MedFlamingo, a medical domain-specific Vision-Language Model, with PPO. Our method is evaluated on five diverse laparoscopic surgery task environments in LapGym, using only endoscopic visual observations. MedFlamingo PPO outperforms and converges faster compared to both standard vision-based PPO and OpenFlamingo PPO baselines, achieving task success rates exceeding 70% across all environments, with improvements ranging from 66.67% to 1114.29% compared to baseline. By processing task observations and instructions once per episode to generate high-level planning tokens, our method efficiently combines medical expertise with real-time visual feedback. Our results highlight the value of specialized medical knowledge in robotic surgical planning and decision-making."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"IEEE CAI 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06064","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:32Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:03:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06056","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06056","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06056","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scientific Algorithm Discovery by Augmenting AlphaEvolve with Deep Research"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:49:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models hold promise as scientific assistants, yet existing agents either rely solely on algorithm evolution or on deep research in isolation, both of which face critical limitations. Pure algorithm evolution, as in AlphaEvolve, depends only on the internal knowledge of LLMs and quickly plateaus in complex domains, while pure deep research proposes ideas without validation, resulting in unrealistic or unimplementable solutions. We present DeepEvolve, an agent that integrates deep research with algorithm evolution, uniting external knowledge retrieval, cross-file code editing, and systematic debugging under a feedback-driven iterative loop. Each iteration not only proposes new hypotheses but also refines, implements, and tests them, avoiding both shallow improvements and unproductive over-refinements. Across nine benchmarks in chemistry, mathematics, biology, materials, and patents, DeepEvolve consistently improves the initial algorithm, producing executable new algorithms with sustained gains. By bridging the gap between unguided evolution and research without grounding, DeepEvolve provides a reliable framework for advancing scientific algorithm discovery. Our code is available at https://github.com/liugangcode/deepevolve."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 17 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06056","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:21Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:03:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06040","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06040","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06040","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinye","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Xinye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongcan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Hongcan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawen","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Jiawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoshun","familyName":"Nan","name":"Nan, Guoshun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqi","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Yuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianhao","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Tianhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaojuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VideoMiner: Iteratively Grounding Key Frames of Hour-Long Videos via Tree-based Group Relative Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:34:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:55:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding hour-long videos with multi-modal large language models (MM-LLMs) enriches the landscape of human-centered AI applications. However, for end-to-end video understanding with LLMs, uniformly sampling video frames results in LLMs being overwhelmed by a vast amount of irrelevant information as video length increases. Existing hierarchical key frame extraction methods improve the accuracy of video understanding but still face two critical challenges. 1) How can the interference of extensive redundant information in long videos be mitigated? 2) How can a model dynamically adapt to complex hierarchical structures while accurately identifying key frames? To address these issues, we propose VideoMiner, which iteratively segments, captions, and clusters long videos, forming a hierarchical tree structure. The proposed VideoMiner progresses from long videos to events to frames while preserving temporal coherence, effectively addressing the first challenge. To precisely locate key frames, we introduce T-GRPO, a tree-based group relative policy optimization in reinforcement learning method that guides the exploration of the VideoMiner. The proposed T-GRPO is specifically designed for tree structures, integrating spatiotemporal information at the event level while being guided by the question, thus solving the second challenge. We achieve superior performance in all long-video understanding tasks and uncover several interesting insights. Our proposed T-GRPO surprisingly incentivizes the model to spontaneously generate a reasoning chain. Additionally, the designed tree growth auxin dynamically adjusts the expansion depth, obtaining accuracy and efficiency gains. The code is publicly available at https://github.com/caoxinye/VideoMiner."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06040","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:02:58Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:02:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:02:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06035","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06035","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06035","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ondřej","familyName":"Týbl","name":"Týbl, Ondřej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukáš","familyName":"Neumann","name":"Neumann, Lukáš","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Universal Neural Architecture Space: Covering ConvNets, Transformers and Everything in Between"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:31:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:55:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Universal Neural Architecture Space (UniNAS), a generic search space for neural architecture search (NAS) which unifies convolutional networks, transformers, and their hybrid architectures under a single, flexible framework. Our approach enables discovery of novel architectures as well as analyzing existing architectures in a common framework. We also propose a new search algorithm that allows traversing the proposed search space, and demonstrate that the space contains interesting architectures, which, when using identical training setup, outperform state-of-the-art hand-crafted architectures. Finally, a unified toolkit including a standardized training and evaluation protocol is introduced to foster reproducibility and enable fair comparison in NAS research. Overall, this work opens a pathway towards systematically exploring the full spectrum of neural architectures with a unified graph-based NAS perspective."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06035","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:02:51Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:02:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:02:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06018","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06018","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06018","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy","familyName":"Pistotti","name":"Pistotti, Timothy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Brown","name":"Brown, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Witbrock","name":"Witbrock, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating The Impact of Stimulus Quality in Investigations of LLM Language Performance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:16:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:54:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent studies employing Large Language Models (LLMs) to test the Argument from the Poverty of the Stimulus (APS) have yielded contrasting results across syntactic phenomena. This paper investigates the hypothesis that characteristics of the stimuli used in recent studies, including lexical ambiguities and structural complexities, may confound model performance. A methodology is proposed for re-evaluating LLM competence on syntactic prediction, focusing on GPT-2. This involves: 1) establishing a baseline on previously used (both filtered and unfiltered) stimuli, and 2) generating a new, refined dataset using a state-of-the-art (SOTA) generative LLM (Gemini 2.5 Pro Preview) guided by linguistically-informed templates designed to mitigate identified confounds. Our preliminary findings indicate that GPT-2 demonstrates notably improved performance on these refined PG stimuli compared to baselines, suggesting that stimulus quality significantly influences outcomes in surprisal-based evaluations of LLM syntactic competency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Presented at https://brigap-workshop.github.io/ Information to be updated upon publication of proceedings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06018","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:02:27Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:02:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:02:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06001","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06001","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06001","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy","familyName":"Pistotti","name":"Pistotti, Timothy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Brown","name":"Brown, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Witbrock","name":"Witbrock, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring Gaps in the APS: Direct Minimal Pair Analysis in LLM Syntactic Assessments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:03:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:53:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent studies probing the Argument from the Poverty of the Stimulus (APS) have applied Large Language Models (LLMs) to test the learnability of complex syntax through surprisal-based metrics. However, divergent conclusions raise questions concerning the insights these metrics offer. While Wilcox et al. (2024) used direct minimal pair comparisons (the \"wh-effect\") to demonstrate that models successfully generalise knowledge of filler-gap dependencies, Lan et al. (2024) used a Difference-in-Differences (DiD) metric and found that models largely fail on parasitic gaps (PGs). This paper argues that the direct minimal pair approach offers greater diagnostic transparency. We demonstrate this by generating a full 8-permutation paradigm of refined PG stimuli and evaluating the GPT-2 model used in previous studies with a systematic Wilcox-style wh-effect analysis. Our results show that GPT-2 succeeds across all four tested conditions, indicating robust knowledge of filler-gap licensing principles even in complex PG environments. This finding, which contrasts with the more ambiguous results from DiD-style metrics, suggests that the choice of evaluation metric is critical for assessing an LLM's syntactic competence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Presented at the https://brigap-workshop.github.io/ Information to be updated after publication of proceedings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06001","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:02:03Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:02:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:02:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05987","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05987","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05987","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueyan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xueyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guinan","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Guinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mrinmaya","familyName":"Sachan","name":"Sachan, Mrinmaya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Geiping","name":"Geiping, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sample Smart, Not Hard: Correctness-First Decoding for Better Reasoning in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:46:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:52:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly applied to complex tasks that require extended reasoning. In such settings, models often benefit from diverse chains-of-thought to arrive at multiple candidate solutions. This requires two competing objectives: to inject enough stochasticity to explore multiple reasoning chains, and to ensure sufficient accuracy and quality in each path. Existing works pursue the first objective by increasing exploration at highly uncertain steps with higher temperature or larger candidate token sets, while others improve reliability by rejecting samples with low confidence post-generation, implying that low confidence correlates with low answer quality. These two lines of thought are in conflict, as they conflate different sources of uncertainty. To resolve this, we argue that the decoding rule should be calibrated by correctness, not confidence alone. We should sample from tokens with higher estimated correctness, and reduce sampling where expected correctness is low. We propose simple strategies that achieve this goal: Greedy-Threshold makes sampling greedy at very low confidence steps. Calibrated-TopK and Calibrated-epsilon set truncation threshold based on estimated rank-wise correctness. Together, our findings challenge prevailing heuristics about decoding under uncertainty and show gains across math and general reasoning benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05987","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:44Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:01:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05976","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05976","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05976","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eashan","familyName":"Adhikarla","name":"Adhikarla, Eashan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian D.","familyName":"Davison","name":"Davison, Brian D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diffusion Models for Low-Light Image Enhancement: A Multi-Perspective Taxonomy and Performance Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:30:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:51:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Low-light image enhancement (LLIE) is vital for safety-critical applications such as surveillance, autonomous navigation, and medical imaging, where visibility degradation can impair downstream task performance. Recently, diffusion models have emerged as a promising generative paradigm for LLIE due to their capacity to model complex image distributions via iterative denoising. This survey provides an up-to-date critical analysis of diffusion models for LLIE, distinctively featuring an in-depth comparative performance evaluation against Generative Adversarial Network and Transformer-based state-of-the-art methods, a thorough examination of practical deployment challenges, and a forward-looking perspective on the role of emerging paradigms like foundation models. We propose a multi-perspective taxonomy encompassing six categories: Intrinsic Decomposition, Spectral \u0026amp; Latent, Accelerated, Guided, Multimodal, and Autonomous; that map enhancement methods across physical priors, conditioning schemes, and computational efficiency. Our taxonomy is grounded in a hybrid view of both the model mechanism and the conditioning signals. We evaluate qualitative failure modes, benchmark inconsistencies, and trade-offs between interpretability, generalization, and inference efficiency. We also discuss real-world deployment constraints (e.g., memory, energy use) and ethical considerations. This survey aims to guide the next generation of diffusion-based LLIE research by highlighting trends and surfacing open research questions, including novel conditioning, real-time adaptation, and the potential of foundation models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05976","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:28Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:01:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05972","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05972","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05972","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Periklis","familyName":"Mantenoglou","name":"Mantenoglou, Periklis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishi","familyName":"Hazra","name":"Hazra, Rishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro Zuidberg Dos","familyName":"Martires","name":"Martires, Pedro Zuidberg Dos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luc","familyName":"De Raedt","name":"De Raedt, Luc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LexiCon: a Benchmark for Planning under Temporal Constraints in Natural Language"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:28:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:51:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Owing to their reasoning capabilities, large language models (LLMs) have been evaluated on planning tasks described in natural language. However, LLMs have largely been tested on planning domains without constraints. In order to deploy them in real-world settings where adherence to constraints, in particular safety constraints, is critical, we need to evaluate their performance on constrained planning tasks. We introduce LexiCon -- a natural language-based (Lexi) constrained (Con) planning benchmark, consisting of a suite of environments, that can be used to evaluate the planning capabilities of LLMs in a principled fashion. The core idea behind LexiCon is to take existing planning environments and impose temporal constraints on the states. These constrained problems are then translated into natural language and given to an LLM to solve. A key feature of LexiCon is its extensibility. That is, the set of supported environments can be extended with new (unconstrained) environment generators, for which temporal constraints are constructed automatically. This renders LexiCon future-proof: the hardness of the generated planning problems can be increased as the planning capabilities of LLMs improve. Our experiments reveal that the performance of state-of-the-art LLMs, including reasoning models like GPT-5, o3, and R1, deteriorates as the degree of constrainedness of the planning tasks increases."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05972","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:22Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:01:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05968","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05968","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05968","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Scott","familyName":"Frees","name":"Frees, Scott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Extending ResourceLink: Patterns for Large Dataset Processing in MCP Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:23:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:51:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models translate natural language into database queries, yet context window limitations prevent direct deployment in reporting systems where complete datasets exhaust available tokens. The Model Context Protocol specification defines ResourceLink for referencing external resources, but practical patterns for implementing scalable reporting architectures remain undocumented. This paper presents patterns for building LLM-powered reporting systems that decouple query generation from data retrieval. We introduce a dual-response pattern extending ResourceLink to support both iterative query refinement and out-of-band data access, accompanied by patterns for multi-tenant security and resource lifecycle management. These patterns address fundamental challenges in LLM-driven reporting applications and provide practical guidance for developers building them."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05968","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:16Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:01:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05943","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05943","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05943","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheyue","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Zheyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mustapha","familyName":"Abdullahi","name":"Abdullahi, Mustapha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuo","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Tuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huining","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Huining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zelai","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zelai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boxun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Boxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EARL: Efficient Agentic Reinforcement Learning Systems for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:52:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:49:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has become a pivotal component of large language model (LLM) post-training, and agentic RL extends this paradigm to operate as agents through multi-turn interaction and tool use. Scaling such systems exposes two practical bottlenecks: (1) context length grows rapidly during training, inflating memory usage and latency, and triggering out-of-memory (OOM) failures; and (2) intermediate tensors accumulate with context length, making cross-device data movement a major system bottleneck.\n We present EARL, a scalable system for efficient agentic RL. EARL designs a parallelism selector that dynamically adapts model and training parallelism across RL stages based on sequence length and system load, and a data dispatcher that performs layout-aware, decentralized exchange of intermediate data batches. Together, these components increase throughput, reduce long-context failures, and enable stable large-scale training of agentic LLMs without relying on hard limits or penalties of context length."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05943","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:00:41Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:00:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:00:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05891","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05891","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05891","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanran","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingyao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Bingyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenzhao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Wenzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueqi","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Yueqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiwen","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"$\\bf{D^3}$QE: Learning Discrete Distribution Discrepancy-aware Quantization Error for Autoregressive-Generated Image Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:02:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:46:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The emergence of visual autoregressive (AR) models has revolutionized image generation while presenting new challenges for synthetic image detection. Unlike previous GAN or diffusion-based methods, AR models generate images through discrete token prediction, exhibiting both marked improvements in image synthesis quality and unique characteristics in their vector-quantized representations. In this paper, we propose to leverage Discrete Distribution Discrepancy-aware Quantization Error (D$^3$QE) for autoregressive-generated image detection that exploits the distinctive patterns and the frequency distribution bias of the codebook existing in real and fake images. We introduce a discrete distribution discrepancy-aware transformer that integrates dynamic codebook frequency statistics into its attention mechanism, fusing semantic features and quantization error latent. To evaluate our method, we construct a comprehensive dataset termed ARForensics covering 7 mainstream visual AR models. Experiments demonstrate superior detection accuracy and strong generalization of D$^3$QE across different AR models, with robustness to real-world perturbations. Code is available at \\href{https://github.com/Zhangyr2022/D3QE}{https://github.com/Zhangyr2022/D3QE}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 5 figures, published to ICCV2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05891","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:59:25Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:59:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:59:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05869","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05869","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05869","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The fragility of \"cultural tendencies\" in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:37:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:45:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In a recent study, Lu, Song, and Zhang (2025) (LSZ) propose that large language models (LLMs), when prompted in different languages, display culturally specific tendencies. They report that the two models (i.e., GPT and ERNIE) respond in more interdependent and holistic ways when prompted in Chinese, and more independent and analytic ways when prompted in English. LSZ attribute these differences to deep-seated cultural patterns in the models, claiming that prompt language alone can induce substantial cultural shifts. While we acknowledge the empirical patterns they observed, we find their experiments, methods, and interpretations problematic. In this paper, we critically re-evaluate the methodology, theoretical framing, and conclusions of LSZ. We argue that the reported \"cultural tendencies\" are not stable traits but fragile artifacts of specific models and task design. To test this, we conducted targeted replications using a broader set of LLMs and a larger number of test items. Our results show that prompt language has minimal effect on outputs, challenging LSZ's claim that these models encode grounded cultural beliefs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05869","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:58:55Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:58:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:58:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05836","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05836","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05836","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ruyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangkun","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Shangkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Flow4Agent: Long-form Video Understanding via Motion Prior from Optical Flow"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:01:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:43:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-form video understanding has always been a challenging problem due to the significant redundancy in both temporal and spatial contents. This challenge is further exacerbated by the limited context length of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs). To address this issue, many previous works have attempted to extract key video information, where the \"key\" is typically semantic-aware and heavily dependent on the CLIP model as prior. In this paper, we propose Flow4Agent, a novel framework that pioneeringly incorporates motion priors from optical flow to facilitate LLM-based long video understanding. Flow4Agent mitigates the redundancy in long videos at both temporal and spatial levels through two core modules: Temporal Granularity Optimization (TGO) adaptively refines framelevel hierarchies, which first leverages coarse flow priors to group similar visual contents and then applies semantic priors to filter out highly irrelevant scene information. Motion Token Pruning (MTP) further refines the intra-frame visual representations, pruning high-redundancy video tokens using fine-grained optical flow information. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our Flow4Agent outperforms existing methods across a wide range of video MLLM benchmarks, especially for hour-level video understanding tasks, achieving 64.7% on Video-MME, 71.4% on MLVU and 60.4% on LongVideoBench."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICCV' 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05836","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:58:08Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:58:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:58:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05828","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05828","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05828","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Marinoni","name":"Marinoni, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riccardo Fosco","familyName":"Gramaccioni","name":"Gramaccioni, Riccardo Fosco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuki","familyName":"Shimada","name":"Shimada, Kazuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takashi","familyName":"Shibuya","name":"Shibuya, Takashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuki","familyName":"Mitsufuji","name":"Mitsufuji, Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danilo","familyName":"Comminiello","name":"Comminiello, Danilo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StereoSync: Spatially-Aware Stereo Audio Generation from Video"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T11:51:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:42:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although audio generation has been widely studied over recent years, video-aligned audio generation still remains a relatively unexplored frontier. To address this gap, we introduce StereoSync, a novel and efficient model designed to generate audio that is both temporally synchronized with a reference video and spatially aligned with its visual context. Moreover, StereoSync also achieves efficiency by leveraging pretrained foundation models, reducing the need for extensive training while maintaining high-quality synthesis. Unlike existing methods that primarily focus on temporal synchronization, StereoSync introduces a significant advancement by incorporating spatial awareness into video-aligned audio generation. Indeed, given an input video, our approach extracts spatial cues from depth maps and bounding boxes, using them as cross-attention conditioning in a diffusion-based audio generation model. Such an approach allows StereoSync to go beyond simple synchronization, producing stereo audio that dynamically adapts to the spatial structure and movement of a video scene. We evaluate StereoSync on Walking The Maps, a curated dataset comprising videos from video games that feature animated characters walking through diverse environments. Experimental results demonstrate the ability of StereoSync to achieve both temporal and spatial alignment, advancing the state of the art in video-to-audio generation and resulting in a significantly more immersive and realistic audio experience."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at IJCNN 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05828","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:57:57Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:57:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:57:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05827","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05827","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05827","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuanghao","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Shuanghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanqi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wanqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuedi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuedi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengxiang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Pengxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donglin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Donglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Badong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Badong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VCoT-Grasp: Grasp Foundation Models with Visual Chain-of-Thought Reasoning for Language-driven Grasp Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T11:50:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:42:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robotic grasping is one of the most fundamental tasks in robotic manipulation, and grasp detection/generation has long been the subject of extensive research. Recently, language-driven grasp generation has emerged as a promising direction due to its practical interaction capabilities. However, most existing approaches either lack sufficient reasoning and generalization capabilities or depend on complex modular pipelines. Moreover, current grasp foundation models tend to overemphasize dialog and object semantics, resulting in inferior performance and restriction to single-object grasping. To maintain strong reasoning ability and generalization in cluttered environments, we propose VCoT-Grasp, an end-to-end grasp foundation model that incorporates visual chain-of-thought reasoning to enhance visual understanding for grasp generation. VCoT-Grasp adopts a multi-turn processing paradigm that dynamically focuses on visual inputs while providing interpretable reasoning traces. For training, we refine and introduce a large-scale dataset, VCoT-GraspSet, comprising 167K synthetic images with over 1.36M grasps, as well as 400+ real-world images with more than 1.2K grasps, annotated with intermediate bounding boxes. Extensive experiments on both VCoT-GraspSet and real robot demonstrate that our method significantly improves grasp success rates and generalizes effectively to unseen objects, backgrounds, and distractors. More details can be found at https://zhanghr2001.github.io/VCoT-Grasp.github.io."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05827","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:57:55Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:57:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:57:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05826","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05826","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05826","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pubudu L.","familyName":"Indrasiri","name":"Indrasiri, Pubudu L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bipasha","familyName":"Kashyap","name":"Kashyap, Bipasha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pubudu N.","familyName":"Pathirana","name":"Pathirana, Pubudu N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Leveraging Vision Transformers for Enhanced Classification of Emotions using ECG Signals"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T11:49:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:42:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Biomedical signals provide insights into various conditions affecting the human body. Beyond diagnostic capabilities, these signals offer a deeper understanding of how specific organs respond to an individual's emotions and feelings. For instance, ECG data can reveal changes in heart rate variability linked to emotional arousal, stress levels, and autonomic nervous system activity. This data offers a window into the physiological basis of our emotional states. Recent advancements in the field diverge from conventional approaches by leveraging the power of advanced transformer architectures, which surpass traditional machine learning and deep learning methods. We begin by assessing the effectiveness of the Vision Transformer (ViT), a forefront model in image classification, for identifying emotions in imaged ECGs. Following this, we present and evaluate an improved version of ViT, integrating both CNN and SE blocks, aiming to bolster performance on imaged ECGs associated with emotion detection. Our method unfolds in two critical phases: first, we apply advanced preprocessing techniques for signal purification and converting signals into interpretable images using continuous wavelet transform and power spectral density analysis; second, we unveil a performance-boosted vision transformer architecture, cleverly enhanced with convolutional neural network components, to adeptly tackle the challenges of emotion recognition. Our methodology's robustness and innovation were thoroughly tested using ECG data from the YAAD and DREAMER datasets, leading to remarkable outcomes. For the YAAD dataset, our approach outperformed existing state-of-the-art methods in classifying seven unique emotional states, as well as in valence and arousal classification. Similarly, in the DREAMER dataset, our method excelled in distinguishing between valence, arousal and dominance, surpassing current leading techniques."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05826","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:57:54Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:57:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:57:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05781","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05781","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05781","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runxi","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Runxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucheng","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingguo","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Qingguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongxian","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Yongxian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chun","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Chun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yelong","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yelong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weizhu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Weizhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeyun","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Yeyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mixture of Neuron Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:51:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:40:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, we first explore whether the parameters activated by the MoE layer remain highly sparse at inference. We perform a sparsification study on several representative MoE models. For each expert, we rank parameters by the magnitude of their activations from the gate projection and progressively prune the activated subset. Pruning up to 60% of parameters within that subset causes only negligible task-performance degradation; substantial drops occur only after more than 90% are removed. We further decompose experts into neuron-granular MoE and visualize their activation values, finding that most neuron activations are near zero. This observation motivates us to select only high-activation neuron experts during pretraining. Based on this insight, we propose Mixture of Neuron Experts (MoNE). MoNE achieves neuron-granular expert selection by only applying a simple top-k selection within each expert, incurs negligible latency, and requires no additional routing parameters or inter-expert communication. Extensive experiments demonstrate that MoNE matches traditional MoE performance while activating only 50% of the MoE-layer parameters, and it consistently outperforms traditional MoE when compared at equal numbers of activated parameters. These results suggest that MoNE is a practical approach to improving parameter utilization and inference efficiency in MoE-like models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 page, 11 figures, 7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05781","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:56:51Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:56:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:56:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05756","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05756","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05756","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aleksandr","familyName":"Lukoianov","name":"Lukoianov, Aleksandr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anssi","familyName":"Klapuri","name":"Klapuri, Anssi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Transcribing Rhythmic Patterns of the Guitar Track in Polyphonic Music"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:22:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:39:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Whereas chord transcription has received considerable attention during the past couple of decades, far less work has been devoted to transcribing and encoding the rhythmic patterns that occur in a song. The topic is especially relevant for instruments such as the rhythm guitar, which is typically played by strumming rhythmic patterns that repeat and vary over time. However, in many cases one cannot objectively define a single \"right\" rhythmic pattern for a given song section. To create a dataset with well-defined ground-truth labels, we asked expert musicians to transcribe the rhythmic patterns in 410 popular songs and record cover versions where the guitar tracks followed those transcriptions. To transcribe the strums and their corresponding rhythmic patterns, we propose a three-step framework. Firstly, we perform approximate stem separation to extract the guitar part from the polyphonic mixture. Secondly, we detect individual strums within the separated guitar audio, using a pre-trained foundation model (MERT) as a backbone. Finally, we carry out a pattern-decoding process in which the transcribed sequence of guitar strums is represented by patterns drawn from an expert-curated vocabulary. We show that it is possible to transcribe the rhythmic patterns of the guitar track in polyphonic music with quite high accuracy, producing a representation that is human-readable and includes automatically detected bar lines and time signature markers. We perform ablation studies and error analysis and propose a set of evaluation metrics to assess the accuracy and readability of the predicted rhythmic pattern sequence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to WASPAA 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05756","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:56:16Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:56:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:56:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":310,"totalPages":4,"page":2},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-07T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-08T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+Transformer+OR+Transformers+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=3\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-07T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-08T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+Transformer+OR+Transformers+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29"}}